WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.750
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4534.

2
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.500
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.750
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Today is October thirty-first, 2015.

4
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.250
I'm Chelsea Arseneault with the [LSU Libraries] T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History,

5
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.000
and I'm here today with Mr. Mickey . . . Is it Hubert?

6
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:09.354
<v HUBER SMITH>Huber.

7
00:00:09.354 --> 00:00:09.707
<v ARSENEAULT>Huber.

8
00:00:09.707 --> 00:00:10.768
Mr. Mikey Huber.

9
00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:11.476
<v HUBER>No T.

10
00:00:11.476 --> 00:00:18.195
<v ARSENEAULT>No T. At the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on the Old Spanish Trail to talk about his memories of Mossville,

11
00:00:18.195 --> 00:00:25.268
Louisiana, as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

12
00:00:25.268 --> 00:00:31.634
Mr. Mikey, we want to thank you for taking your time to share your story with us today.

13
00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:36.232
So for the record, for the recording, can you state your full name?

14
00:00:36.232 --> 00:00:38.000
<v SMITH>Huber “Mickey” Smith Senior.

15
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.724
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

16
00:00:38.724 --> 00:00:42.343
And is Mickey a nickname?

17
00:00:42.343 --> 00:00:44.514
<v SMITH>Yes it is.

18
00:00:44.514 --> 00:00:48.133
<v ARSENEAULT>How'd you get that nickname?

19
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:51.029
<v SMITH>I really don't know.

20
00:00:51.029 --> 00:00:56.819
Mickey from as long as I can remember.

21
00:00:56.819 --> 00:01:04.057
My grandparents started out raising me in the early days.

22
00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:06.952
It was really hard.

23
00:01:06.952 --> 00:01:12.019
Most of the family had to work.

24
00:01:12.019 --> 00:01:17.810
So anyway, it was just a big family.

25
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:23.600
All the children stayed at the grandparents' house.

26
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.219
We had land and everything,

27
00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:43.867
but we didn't have sufficient housing because the children at that time stayed . . . So we stayed as a family with my mother's parents.

28
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:44.590
<v ARSENEAULT>When?

29
00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.657
<v SMITH>Until during the war, '41 or '42.

30
00:01:49.657 --> 00:01:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So when were you born?

31
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.651
<v SMITH>Born March fourth, 1934.

32
00:01:56.651 --> 00:01:59.302
<v ARSENEAULT>And where was that?

33
00:01:59.302 --> 00:02:00.628
<v SMITH>In Mossville.

34
00:02:00.628 --> 00:02:05.930
Sometimes they call it Mossville, sometimes Westlake [Louisiana].

35
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:13.221
<c.p>Didn't have a really detailed place as to where you are.

36
00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:30.453
If you was in Mossville you is part of what is going on in Mossville and you went the extent . . . I went to school in Westlake.

37
00:02:30.453 --> 00:02:34.430
So kind of hard to say.

38
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.081
I just said whatever.

39
00:02:37.081 --> 00:02:38.407
Westlake, Mossville.

40
00:02:38.407 --> 00:02:43.047
Mossville though during that period of time.

41
00:02:43.047 --> 00:02:47.023
And during the war about '45.

42
00:02:47.023 --> 00:02:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that World War II?

43
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.685
<v SMITH>World War II.

44
00:02:52.685 --> 00:02:55.493
Family began to move about.

45
00:02:55.493 --> 00:03:02.232
My parents went to California to work during that period of time,

46
00:03:02.232 --> 00:03:11.779
and so I stayed here with my grandparents while my parents went to work out in California.

47
00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:26.380
That went on for quite a period of time after the war, and then everybody finally came back home, what they call home, in Mossville, Westlake.

48
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.627
Began life there again.

49
00:03:28.627 --> 00:03:31.996
That's pretty much what it was.

50
00:03:31.996 --> 00:03:39.297
Didn't have a whole lot to offer for us because we had nothing.

51
00:03:39.297 --> 00:03:49.967
Really wasn't looking for anything but a stable life of being here as a Mossville person or Westlake person.

52
00:03:49.967 --> 00:03:57.830
And this is where we stayed until I grew up to fend for myself.

53
00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:07.938
And that's my history from birth to . . . All of my teen years were right here in Westlake, Mossville.

54
00:04:07.938 --> 00:04:15.239
And I say Westlake, Mossville simply because . . . You say, "Well why that is?"

55
00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:21.978
When you went to the track for whatever reason, that was Mossville.

56
00:04:21.978 --> 00:04:25.909
The K.C.S. [Kansas City Southern] railroad track.

57
00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:35.457
And then there was nothing there but the families we'll talk about later right along that way.

58
00:04:35.457 --> 00:04:39.388
Kind of near a company or refinery.

59
00:04:39.388 --> 00:04:48.935
And so all that we did in that school in that address, and what have you, Westlake.

60
00:04:48.935 --> 00:04:51.743
But we were Mossville people.

61
00:04:51.743 --> 00:05:04.098
I guess family divided themselves up into little squatting places or whatever you want to call it, but we always addressed Westlake.

62
00:05:04.098 --> 00:05:08.591
And I think most of Mossville did too.

63
00:05:08.591 --> 00:05:11.960
I'm really not sure about that.

64
00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:12.522
But

65
00:05:12.522 --> 00:05:26.000
<c.p>anyway we was still whatever went on here it took place in Mossville like this change in this situation we were Mossville also.

66
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.207
I guess what we are at this point is just who we really are.

67
00:05:33.207 --> 00:05:36.810
That was not much education and stuff.

68
00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:42.987
We were who your parents say you was and where you lived.

69
00:05:42.987 --> 00:05:51.224
This is what we were told we was until we grew up to fend for ourselves.

70
00:05:51.224 --> 00:06:01.519
This was still home and most of the people my age we left here and went different places to work.

71
00:06:01.519 --> 00:06:04.093
We always come back home.

72
00:06:04.093 --> 00:06:15.418
It's maybe a little premature, but I stayed away from here a long time and finally I come back home to live.

73
00:06:15.418 --> 00:06:24.684
One reason that I did in the '50s come back to live is because to help with Mother.

74
00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:36.523
She had got kind of [over the fence?] and help ourselves and that's one reason that I came back and I stayed here.

75
00:06:36.523 --> 00:06:41.671
Then after a long period of time I'm still here.

76
00:06:41.671 --> 00:06:49.392
Not because of that, it's because I choose to be home, and this is home.

77
00:06:49.392 --> 00:06:59.688
I see all the changes and the growth and what and down treading as I would say with Mossville leaving.

78
00:06:59.688 --> 00:07:02.776
Kind of a hard thing itself.

79
00:07:02.776 --> 00:07:07.409
I don't think I’ll ever accept that Mossville's gone.

80
00:07:07.409 --> 00:07:10.498
This is really a conditional thing.

81
00:07:10.498 --> 00:07:16.160
It’s helpful to the community and the people and this work.

82
00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.308
It's what it takes to do what we doing now.

83
00:07:21.308 --> 00:07:28.000
But I really could never picture this as getting rid of Mossville.

84
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:35.588
As I come through here sometimes, some days, and I look and see what was what,

85
00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:46.495
where with these houses and the people that's not there anymore, it's kind of hard to bring it all together to accept it.

86
00:07:46.495 --> 00:07:47.918
But it's here.

87
00:07:47.918 --> 00:07:53.608
Sasol [South African energy and chemical company], the work that they brought.

88
00:07:53.608 --> 00:07:57.876
The way that they're changing the roads and things.

89
00:07:57.876 --> 00:08:06.887
It's kind of hard to accept that this is real because you see it riding through looking for Mossville.

90
00:08:06.887 --> 00:08:11.155
You still riding through here going to Sulphur [Louisiana].

91
00:08:11.155 --> 00:08:15.423
You're still coming back going to Westlake and on.

92
00:08:15.423 --> 00:08:22.536
I don't know how it will fit in and what it'll do mentally to you.

93
00:08:22.536 --> 00:08:25.856
It's our home that's home no more.

94
00:08:25.856 --> 00:08:33.443
Belongs to the companies that's taking over and building and doing what they doing to improve

95
00:08:33.443 --> 00:08:35.340
<c.p>our work and living.

96
00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.608
It belongs to them and not to us anymore.

97
00:08:39.608 --> 00:08:47.670
For me there'll always be memories of who used to live here, who used to live there.

98
00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:53.361
The going and coming of people that you know is no more.

99
00:08:53.361 --> 00:08:54.309
No longer.

100
00:08:54.309 --> 00:09:00.474
It's going to take its toll on you because it won't grow back.

101
00:09:00.474 --> 00:09:04.268
It's rolling forward and we can see it.

102
00:09:04.268 --> 00:09:10.907
But during the war, World War II, people left here in the same way.

103
00:09:10.907 --> 00:09:18.969
They left here and the ones that didn't go to the war, didn’t fight in the war,

104
00:09:18.969 --> 00:09:27.031
they left here and went to where they could find work because there was no work here.

105
00:09:27.031 --> 00:09:32.247
The saw mills before my time is really what it was.

106
00:09:32.247 --> 00:09:41.258
Then finally after they cut out all the sawmilling and left went back to what it used to be.

107
00:09:41.258 --> 00:09:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why did people stop working at the saw mill?

108
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:53.273
<v SMITH>Well they used up all the timber there was just like flat land.

109
00:09:53.273 --> 00:09:58.867
It . . . Sawmill here . . . People that went to the armed services,

110
00:09:58.867 --> 00:10:05.021
say fifty percent of them never came back here to live.

111
00:10:05.021 --> 00:10:09.497
Live somewhere else and made their home there.

112
00:10:09.497 --> 00:10:14.531
They passed on most of them since the war.

113
00:10:14.531 --> 00:10:16.769
That changed a lot.

114
00:10:16.769 --> 00:10:22.364
Just people we saw and knew and raised up with.

115
00:10:22.364 --> 00:10:28.517
After the war and whatever we didn't see them no more.

116
00:10:28.517 --> 00:10:30.755
Just heard of them.

117
00:10:30.755 --> 00:10:35.231
They lived in other places of the world.

118
00:10:35.231 --> 00:10:37.469
They never came back.

119
00:10:37.469 --> 00:10:48.098
Mossville and Westlake was still the same as to what it stood for and our children and the grandchildren.

120
00:10:48.098 --> 00:10:49.217
They knew.

121
00:10:49.217 --> 00:11:06.000
I made one and I can relate back to people that haven't been seen since I went to school with them in that period of time and where we are.

122
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:16.070
Even in doing this I can think of names of people that I don't know where they went.

123
00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:23.902
Wasn't that close to keep up with this person being in a certain location.

124
00:11:23.902 --> 00:11:28.937
But once in a while someone will come home.

125
00:11:28.937 --> 00:11:33.972
I live here and I never quit coming home.

126
00:11:33.972 --> 00:11:37.888
Always come home to see my family.

127
00:11:37.888 --> 00:11:42.923
Of course, it was larger but now it's my

128
00:11:42.923 --> 00:11:44.601
<c.p>children and grandchildren.

129
00:11:44.601 --> 00:11:47.399
Them others, they passed on.

130
00:11:47.399 --> 00:11:51.315
Wouldn't be nobody to come and see.

131
00:11:51.315 --> 00:12:01.944
After your parents pass on and leave you, what we got here and what we know and look at.

132
00:12:01.944 --> 00:12:09.776
But if you could visualize it like I can walking from Sulphur to Westlake.

133
00:12:09.776 --> 00:12:14.252
Quite a task, but thought nothing of it.

134
00:12:14.252 --> 00:12:17.049
Walked from here to Sulphur.

135
00:12:17.049 --> 00:12:20.406
That was the way of life.

136
00:12:20.406 --> 00:12:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it look like when . . . at that time?

137
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:32.456
<v SMITH>Just people who I would have to show you live here and there.

138
00:12:32.456 --> 00:12:37.422
Just at one time this road was a single road.

139
00:12:37.422 --> 00:12:51.327
If two vehicles going east and west was in motion one would have to give the other one half the road until he passed and get back up.

140
00:12:51.327 --> 00:12:54.803
I didn't witness the move of that.

141
00:12:54.803 --> 00:12:57.782
I wasn't here at that time.

142
00:12:57.782 --> 00:12:59.272
I wasn't here.

143
00:12:59.272 --> 00:13:05.231
But finally over the years as I was gone I came back.

144
00:13:05.231 --> 00:13:09.204
But if I just . . . A two-lane road then.

145
00:13:09.204 --> 00:13:16.653
Two cars could travel east and west without having to get over to one side.

146
00:13:16.653 --> 00:13:23.605
Get over close to the ditch to stop and let the other one by.

147
00:13:23.605 --> 00:13:26.088
I never did understand that.

148
00:13:26.088 --> 00:13:34.531
Engineers and people who built bridges and roads and things that they seemed awful ignorant to me.

149
00:13:34.531 --> 00:13:39.000
Seemed awful unnecessary to do something like that.

150
00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:45.867
For example, Lake Charles [Louisiana] streets were so small and they’re like that now.

151
00:13:45.867 --> 00:13:58.129
Until most of the old building that are there are sitting on the edge of what would be another double-lane street that you could use.

152
00:13:58.129 --> 00:14:04.506
You never could do much with downtown because they didn't have the room.

153
00:14:04.506 --> 00:14:18.240
They didn't want to get rid of their older buildings to make room for traffic so they began to move out south of here and what have you.

154
00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:26.087
Like the mall and all that to make things convenient where traffic was a problem then.

155
00:14:26.087 --> 00:14:30.992
Didn't have a problem with traffic problems in the '40s.

156
00:14:30.992 --> 00:14:31.973
With traffic.

157
00:14:31.973 --> 00:14:35.407
There's not many people owned a car.

158
00:14:35.407 --> 00:14:38.350
Now everybody owns three and four.

159
00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:40.312
Your children owns cars.

160
00:14:40.312 --> 00:14:42.764
Something had to be done.

161
00:14:42.764 --> 00:14:55.027
<c.p>It amazes me how the pioneers built Lake Charles and surround it like that and built it under those conditions with no growth for tomorrow.

162
00:14:55.027 --> 00:15:00.422
Lake Charles was built so it would always be like that.

163
00:15:00.422 --> 00:15:08.270
In older pictures that I've seen of tomorrow, it was just built for horse and buggy.

164
00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:09.741
Didn't take pride.

165
00:15:09.741 --> 00:15:11.703
No [hope?] for tomorrow.

166
00:15:11.703 --> 00:15:24.456
Then we got into a war that caused lots of difference and caused people to . . . People had cars who you thought would never have a car.

167
00:15:24.456 --> 00:15:39.171
We always thought it would have been . . . It was always said to be livestock and you had it . . . That's what you worked . . . It went from that after World War II.

168
00:15:39.171 --> 00:15:41.133
It began to grow.

169
00:15:41.133 --> 00:15:43.586
We've always had the land.

170
00:15:43.586 --> 00:15:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why was it growing after World War II?

171
00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.109
<v SMITH>People . . . Migration.

172
00:15:49.109 --> 00:15:52.436
<v ARSENEAULT>What were they . . . Why were they . . .?

173
00:15:52.436 --> 00:15:55.764
<v SMITH>From people . . . Come from different places.

174
00:15:55.764 --> 00:16:11.291
Mostly when they start building these plants and people . . . after they started building the plants and doing the . . . People that were not form here wanted to stay here,

175
00:16:11.291 --> 00:16:13.509
so they bought land.

176
00:16:13.509 --> 00:16:22.936
They began to do some development on that and kind of help out with what we had.

177
00:16:22.936 --> 00:16:30.700
People who engineered a great part . . . like they didn't . . . They just didn't have it.

178
00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:34.027
I don't think they wanted it.

179
00:16:34.027 --> 00:16:40.682
I don't think they wanted it because we never had a supermarket.

180
00:16:40.682 --> 00:16:42.900
All we had was . . .

181
00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.673
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn't want the development?

182
00:16:45.673 --> 00:16:49.000
It that what you mean?

183
00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.366
<v SMITH>No, no . . . Yes.

184
00:16:50.366 --> 00:17:00.837
I mean as far as new stores coming, because every individual that had a local grocery store on here and there and Westlake,

185
00:17:00.837 --> 00:17:05.844
he had a . . . he had kind of a hook in you.

186
00:17:05.844 --> 00:17:12.673
You had these people where they was doing a lot of their business was credit.

187
00:17:12.673 --> 00:17:16.770
Get groceries and pay next week or next month.

188
00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:20.412
They didn't want Market Basket [grocery store chain].

189
00:17:20.412 --> 00:17:26.331
I don't want to say Market Basket then, but just look at it.

190
00:17:26.331 --> 00:17:37.712
They didn't want these people coming in here and starting something that would deprive them of what they already had and looked at for years.

191
00:17:37.712 --> 00:17:40.899
You didn't have no say in it.

192
00:17:40.899 --> 00:17:50.914
If you went to the store and bought a bill of groceries just put down what he said [you had to prove?].

193
00:17:50.914 --> 00:17:53.191
You didn't have no bill.

194
00:17:53.191 --> 00:17:55.467
Just . . . It was a book.

195
00:17:55.467 --> 00:18:02.751
When he balanced that book when he went along with it he said that was it.

196
00:18:02.751 --> 00:18:12.311
Well I can imagine how he felt, but that didn't make it right because I'm not sure that he was right.

197
00:18:12.311 --> 00:18:18.685
When I say right I mean I'm not sure that he was totally honest.

198
00:18:18.685 --> 00:18:23.237
That would do for a person that is totally honest.

199
00:18:23.237 --> 00:18:32.798
Christian-hearted person that wouldn't take advantage, but in business you have some of those things where people take advantage of you.

200
00:18:32.798 --> 00:18:35.074
In that type of business.

201
00:18:35.074 --> 00:18:37.350
I'm not saying everybody did.

202
00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:41.447
This is the way it was at that time.

203
00:18:41.447 --> 00:18:42.813
I don't remember . . .

204
00:18:42.813 --> 00:18:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What grocery stores were in Mossville?

205
00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:49.176
<v SMITH>They didn't have one in Mossville.

206
00:18:49.176 --> 00:18:51.294
<v ARSENEAULT>Where'd you get groceries?

207
00:18:51.294 --> 00:18:52.882
<v SMITH>Westlake and Sulphur.

208
00:18:52.882 --> 00:18:53.412
W.T.

209
00:18:53.412 --> 00:18:56.059
Burton, he was a [millionaire?].

210
00:18:56.059 --> 00:18:57.647
He had grocery

211
00:18:57.647 --> 00:18:59.235
<c.p>stores in Sulphur.

212
00:18:59.235 --> 00:19:01.353
He had the banks.

213
00:19:01.353 --> 00:19:02.941
The [ . . .?] Marine Banks.

214
00:19:02.941 --> 00:19:06.647
National bank in Lake Charles and Sulphur.

215
00:19:06.647 --> 00:19:13.529
He was a tycoon in the way that he did business back then.

216
00:19:13.529 --> 00:19:24.118
There wasn't nothing wrong with the way he did business back then because nobody knew anything any better . . . any different.

217
00:19:24.118 --> 00:19:26.235
Where would you go?

218
00:19:26.235 --> 00:19:28.353
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his name?

219
00:19:28.353 --> 00:19:29.941
<v SMITH>William T. Burton.

220
00:19:29.941 --> 00:19:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Burton?

221
00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:31.576
<v SMITH>W.T.

222
00:19:31.576 --> 00:19:32.152
Burton.

223
00:19:32.152 --> 00:19:40.788
He owned Calcasieu Marine National Bank and . . . I can't think of what it is now.

224
00:19:40.788 --> 00:19:42.515
It's been sold.

225
00:19:42.515 --> 00:19:50.000
They were well-off people and they did good business and they did business.

226
00:19:50.000 --> 00:20:00.364
What if you'd been a poor person and think that was right because you didn't know anything else?

227
00:20:00.364 --> 00:20:06.121
Didn't know where else to go, and that was it.

228
00:20:06.121 --> 00:20:06.697
But . . .

229
00:20:06.697 --> 00:20:17.061
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was your physical home in . . .like where . . . Like when you grew up what road was it on?

230
00:20:17.061 --> 00:20:22.818
<v SMITH>On this road up here like you going towards Continental.

231
00:20:22.818 --> 00:20:25.697
<v ARSENEAULT>It's on Old Spanish Trail?

232
00:20:25.697 --> 00:20:28.000
<v SMITH>Old Spanish Trail.

233
00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:29.930
<v ARSENEAULT>Towards . . . Is it Continental now?

234
00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:31.860
Or is it something else?

235
00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:33.404
<v SMITH>Conoco Philips I believe.

236
00:20:33.404 --> 00:20:33.789
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

237
00:20:33.789 --> 00:20:36.105
So it's over the railroad tracks?

238
00:20:36.105 --> 00:20:38.035
<v SMITH>Yes, over the railroad tracks.

239
00:20:38.035 --> 00:20:42.281
Almost up there to the main gate that you go in.

240
00:20:42.281 --> 00:20:44.982
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about your house?

241
00:20:44.982 --> 00:20:45.368
<v SMITH>Pardon?

242
00:20:45.368 --> 00:20:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the house you grew up in?

243
00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.553
<v SMITH>I remember it very well.

244
00:20:52.553 --> 00:20:55.616
There was nothing fancy about it.

245
00:20:55.616 --> 00:20:58.168
It was just three bedrooms.

246
00:20:58.168 --> 00:21:00.211
Just a three-bedroom home.

247
00:21:00.211 --> 00:21:13.484
It sat off the road there and this is . . . We raised mostly what we had except going to the store for stuff like rice and sugar.

248
00:21:13.484 --> 00:21:16.037
Beans and stuff like that.

249
00:21:16.037 --> 00:21:30.332
Everything else we . . . because most of the time we would take from our garden and take it down to the . . . what we use for well and wash it,

250
00:21:30.332 --> 00:21:42.074
basket it, and then my parents would take it to the store on Saturday evening and get what was needed for the house.

251
00:21:42.074 --> 00:21:45.137
As to what we didn't raise.

252
00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:48.200
Soap, stuff, what we didn't raise.

253
00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:53.816
And that was calculated to see what it would come from.

254
00:21:53.816 --> 00:21:54.326
Then

255
00:21:54.326 --> 00:22:00.963
<c.p>this broker would go outside and see what you had on your wagon.

256
00:22:00.963 --> 00:22:14.237
He would count this and put a price on it and then what you owed him he subtracted from it and see where it left you.

257
00:22:14.237 --> 00:22:17.811
<v ARSENEAULT>So you took it to a broker?

258
00:22:17.811 --> 00:22:22.405
<v SMITH>Well no, no. The guy who runs the store.

259
00:22:22.405 --> 00:22:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Just the man who ran the grocery store?

260
00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:27.500
<v SMITH>Yes.

261
00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

262
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:28.500
<v SMITH>Yes.

263
00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:31.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was buying the produce?

264
00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:40.500
<v SMITH>He would buy what we brought and we would buy what we needed to function from the store.

265
00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:44.000
Like we didn't make no toothpaste.

266
00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it wasn't barter.

267
00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:48.250
He actually gave you money and then you . . . Or did he trade you?

268
00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:48.750
<v SMITH>Just trade.

269
00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You just traded goods.

270
00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:51.034
<v SMITH>Just trade.

271
00:22:51.034 --> 00:23:01.897
I don't think it was . . . I don't remember if any money changed hands because sometimes it would be up and down.

272
00:23:01.897 --> 00:23:11.207
Sometimes if we didn't have enough to what we had already taken up, well it just rolled over.

273
00:23:11.207 --> 00:23:18.966
I don't remember anytime that there was any money changed hands that I can remember.

274
00:23:18.966 --> 00:23:31.379
Because you had to get a thing that you didn't raise or make then you had to get feed for your animals, horses, hogs.

275
00:23:31.379 --> 00:23:33.966
They had to be fed.

276
00:23:33.966 --> 00:23:39.655
That’s the reason I said it pretty well balanced itself out.

277
00:23:39.655 --> 00:23:43.276
If it had any legitimacy about it.

278
00:23:43.276 --> 00:23:45.862
Knowing what you were doing.

279
00:23:45.862 --> 00:23:48.448
You were just doing business.

280
00:23:48.448 --> 00:23:52.069
Whatever he said, that's what it was.

281
00:23:52.069 --> 00:23:55.690
Nobody looked for anyway to correct it.

282
00:23:55.690 --> 00:24:06.552
We were up on this hill here, what they call Trousdale Road up here after you get over the railroad track.

283
00:24:06.552 --> 00:24:10.172
We were mostly up on that hill.

284
00:24:10.172 --> 00:24:12.759
It was . . . How many families?

285
00:24:12.759 --> 00:24:13.276
Seven [ . . .?].

286
00:24:13.276 --> 00:24:16.379
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was like a neighborhood?

287
00:24:16.379 --> 00:24:16.897
<v SMITH>[Agrees]

288
00:24:16.897 --> 00:24:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it have a name?

289
00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:23.146
<v SMITH>No, not even a street name.

290
00:24:23.146 --> 00:24:23.671
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

291
00:24:23.671 --> 00:24:29.963
<v SMITH>No, just only street had a name, but it's Old Spanish Trail.

292
00:24:29.963 --> 00:24:35.732
That was the only thing that we knew was a street.

293
00:24:35.732 --> 00:24:39.402
Didn't have nobody lived on no street.

294
00:24:39.402 --> 00:24:47.793
You just pull up if you was a total stranger and ask, "Where does Smith live?"

295
00:24:47.793 --> 00:24:51.463
"He live right up there so far."

296
00:24:51.463 --> 00:24:54.085
No, they didn't have anything.

297
00:24:54.085 --> 00:24:55.659
No trespassing law.

298
00:24:55.659 --> 00:24:57.756
That was his property.

299
00:24:57.756 --> 00:25:03.000
If you needed to go across it you used it.

300
00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:04.049
Cross it.

301
00:25:04.049 --> 00:25:06.671
Not like it is today.

302
00:25:06.671 --> 00:25:12.439
That was no modern machine like a washing machine or dryer.

303
00:25:12.439 --> 00:25:16.110
You were the washing machine and dryer.

304
00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:23.976
Sometimes during droughts or when the water was low . . . when the well was very low,

305
00:25:23.976 --> 00:25:34.988
sometimes we'd haul water either with an animal on a wagon, or we'd tote it because that gully is down there [ . . .?].

306
00:25:34.988 --> 00:25:38.134
You tote water back and forth.

307
00:25:38.134 --> 00:25:46.000
Sometimes they had to do it a lot of times because of the washing.

308
00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:51.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a name for the river where y'all got the water?

309
00:25:51.333 --> 00:25:54.444
<v SMITH>No, no. It was just a gully.

310
00:25:54.444 --> 00:25:56.667
Go down to the gully.

311
00:25:56.667 --> 00:25:59.333
Didn't have no name to it.

312
00:25:59.333 --> 00:26:06.000
Like I said when you have to cross people's property, it didn't make any difference.

313
00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:13.556
Nobody had a fear of these laws like we have today that you can't come through here.

314
00:26:13.556 --> 00:26:16.667
The other side . . . Give me a paper.

315
00:26:16.667 --> 00:26:19.778
<v ARSENEAULT>You want to use the back of . . .

316
00:26:19.778 --> 00:26:22.000
<v SMITH>Yeah, that'd be good.

317
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:23.440
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . your release form here.

318
00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:25.240
Oh you're going to draw.

319
00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:25.600
<v SMITH>Okay.

320
00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:28.840
<v ARSENEAULT>Want to explain for the recording what you're drawing?

321
00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:31.000
<v SMITH>I'm going to draw OST.

322
00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:31.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

323
00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.400
<v SMITH>I'm going to draw OST.

324
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:33.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

325
00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:35.400
The Old Spanish Trail?

326
00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:35.800
<v SMITH>Yeah.

327
00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:36.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

328
00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:40.200
<v SMITH>I'm going to start right here with the railroad tracks.

329
00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

330
00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:41.790
<v SMITH>Okay.

331
00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:50.484
This railroad track is going on in to DeQuincy [Louisiana] and

332
00:26:50.484 --> 00:26:54.435
<c.p>where else I don't know.

333
00:26:54.435 --> 00:26:55.226
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

334
00:26:55.226 --> 00:27:00.758
<v SMITH>But it come from south of here.

335
00:27:00.758 --> 00:27:07.081
There was this gully that come along here.

336
00:27:07.081 --> 00:27:20.516
Okay, and right on this south corner we had a . . . I want to call it a [joint?].

337
00:27:20.516 --> 00:27:26.048
That's all you could say it was.

338
00:27:26.048 --> 00:27:30.000
Just a [beer joint?].

339
00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:30.684
<v ARSENEAULT>A joint?

340
00:27:30.684 --> 00:27:31.026
<v SMITH>Yes.

341
00:27:31.026 --> 00:27:32.053
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a pub?

342
00:27:32.053 --> 00:27:32.395
<v SMITH>Yes.

343
00:27:32.395 --> 00:27:37.868
Well you call it a club now, but it's just a place where they drink beer.

344
00:27:37.868 --> 00:27:38.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a bar.

345
00:27:38.895 --> 00:27:39.921
<v SMITH>A bar, yes.

346
00:27:39.921 --> 00:27:40.263
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

347
00:27:40.263 --> 00:27:40.947
<v SMITH>A [pub?].

348
00:27:40.947 --> 00:27:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Does it have a name?

349
00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:44.505
<v SMITH>Joy Hill.

350
00:27:44.505 --> 00:27:55.790
Next to it, the man that runs it, his mother had an old homestead [Williams?].

351
00:27:55.790 --> 00:28:10.838
Next to it, almost the same property, and next to it on the south side his sister had a home.

352
00:28:10.838 --> 00:28:14.600
Then where we are, Sullivan’s.

353
00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.857
That's my people.

354
00:28:16.857 --> 00:28:21.371
We had a place here now.

355
00:28:21.371 --> 00:28:26.638
To my knowing any abstract, any deed,

356
00:28:26.638 --> 00:28:42.438
no. When the child got grown he wanted to build him a house go on out there and build him one.

357
00:28:42.438 --> 00:28:43.943
That's it.

358
00:28:43.943 --> 00:28:45.448
That's it.

359
00:28:45.448 --> 00:28:49.210
Wasn't nobody deeds and everything.

360
00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:55.981
We had one family and every family had one.

361
00:28:55.981 --> 00:28:59.743
All of us lived here.

362
00:28:59.743 --> 00:29:02.752
This was the Sullivan’s.

363
00:29:02.752 --> 00:29:05.762
That's my mother's people.

364
00:29:05.762 --> 00:29:24.571
Okay, and then on this side of the street . . . On this side of the street there was a church and there was several homes here.

365
00:29:24.571 --> 00:29:27.581
<v ARSENEAULT>What church was that?

366
00:29:27.581 --> 00:29:31.343
<v SMITH>Church of God and Christ.

367
00:29:31.343 --> 00:29:32.095
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

368
00:29:32.095 --> 00:29:45.638
<v SMITH>Okay, and on this side here going back south toward the . . . toward nothing, toward the swamp back there.

369
00:29:45.638 --> 00:29:49.400
These people here were [Egan’s?].

370
00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:55.419
It was . . . They had about three squatters here.

371
00:29:55.419 --> 00:30:08.962
Before coming to Conoco where they had built . . . That was just a pilot plant when they built it.

372
00:30:08.962 --> 00:30:15.733
They didn't know that it would get this big.

373
00:30:15.733 --> 00:30:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of plant was it?

374
00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:22.112
<v SMITH>Conoco Phillips.

375
00:30:22.112 --> 00:30:22.669
<v ARSENEAULT>Oil?

376
00:30:22.669 --> 00:30:23.225
<v SMITH>Beginning.

377
00:30:23.225 --> 00:30:26.562
But it was Continental Oil Company.

378
00:30:26.562 --> 00:30:29.899
<v ARSENEAULT>So it started off as Continental?

379
00:30:29.899 --> 00:30:34.905
<v SMITH>Continental Oil Company and was that for many years.

380
00:30:34.905 --> 00:30:39.911
I don't know when they became connected with Phillips.

381
00:30:39.911 --> 00:30:42.136
It grew from that.

382
00:30:42.136 --> 00:30:48.254
It went all the way . . . Might be able to draw it.

383
00:30:48.254 --> 00:30:53.260
It went all the way to Houston River Road.

384
00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:58.822
That's where the road turns and goes in to Westlake.

385
00:30:58.822 --> 00:31:09.947
Okay, so if I was identifying anything it would be from Houston River Road back there to the railroad tracks.

386
00:31:09.947 --> 00:31:13.284
That's where I'm trying to get.

387
00:31:13.284 --> 00:31:19.959
These are . . . these people who were here that . . . Those people kin folks.

388
00:31:19.959 --> 00:31:21.627
They were cousins.

389
00:31:21.627 --> 00:31:27.189
I don't know about . . . They were cousins, uncles, and nephews.

390
00:31:27.189 --> 00:31:32.751
That was just the way it was on that side.

391
00:31:32.751 --> 00:31:44.432
And everything else after we come to this curve that goes on in to Westlake and then finally to Lake Charles,

392
00:31:44.432 --> 00:31:47.769
that was different, except one thing.

393
00:31:47.769 --> 00:31:52.219
I had to go to school in Westlake.

394
00:31:52.219 --> 00:31:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was this?

395
00:31:55.000 --> 00:32:03.242
<v SMITH>Well I was born in '34 so . . . seven . . . thirty-four . . . What year was that?

396
00:32:03.242 --> 00:32:09.582
All my life . . . That's what we had for a school.

397
00:32:09.582 --> 00:32:13.386
<v ARSENEAULT>There was no school in Mossville.

398
00:32:13.386 --> 00:32:17.824
<v SMITH>Yes, they had a school in Mossville.

399
00:32:17.824 --> 00:32:22.895
It was on this side of the tracks.

400
00:32:22.895 --> 00:32:30.503
Sitting on a portion of this park that we're in here now.

401
00:32:30.503 --> 00:32:38.111
I can't just mark it off because there's so much missing there.

402
00:32:38.111 --> 00:32:41.915
But it sit right along here.

403
00:32:41.915 --> 00:32:43.817
The school here.

404
00:32:43.817 --> 00:32:57.131
Then I don't know why we had a school in Westlake and one in Mossville, but that's the way it was.

405
00:32:57.131 --> 00:32:59.667
That's where we went.

406
00:32:59.667 --> 00:33:06.007
So growing out of all this [ . . .?] growing out of this.

407
00:33:06.007 --> 00:33:09.810
Come to where we begin living.

408
00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:17.418
Maybe like people from the '50s on to where we are now.

409
00:33:17.418 --> 00:33:19.320
No inside toilets.

410
00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:21.856
No help drinking water.

411
00:33:21.856 --> 00:33:26.294
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did people go to the bathroom?

412
00:33:26.294 --> 00:33:26.928
<v SMITH>Pardon?

413
00:33:26.928 --> 00:33:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did people go to the bathroom?

414
00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:36.836
<v SMITH>They dug a little hole out there in the ground.

415
00:33:36.836 --> 00:33:42.156
Put a little building over it and call it the toilet.

416
00:33:42.156 --> 00:33:44.090
That's what it was.

417
00:33:44.090 --> 00:33:46.025
Wasn't no running water.

418
00:33:46.025 --> 00:33:49.410
No inside toilets when I was raised.

419
00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:50.861
Nothing like that.

420
00:33:50.861 --> 00:33:56.664
Just make do with what you had, and that's what they had.

421
00:33:56.664 --> 00:33:59.082
<v ARSENEAULT>Where'd you get the water?

422
00:33:59.082 --> 00:34:07.787
<v SMITH>Dug a hole in the ground and they had some [ . . .?] pipes they put up there down so far.

423
00:34:07.787 --> 00:34:10.205
Dig until you hit water.

424
00:34:10.205 --> 00:34:13.107
That's what you had water for.

425
00:34:13.107 --> 00:34:16.975
Put the bucket on it and draw it

426
00:34:16.975 --> 00:34:20.361
<c.p>like you look on the western pictures.

427
00:34:20.361 --> 00:34:28.098
Put a bucket on the end of it that’ll fit in that and enjoy some water.

428
00:34:28.098 --> 00:34:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How'd you make it drinkable?

429
00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:36.065
<v SMITH>We don't know if it was drinkable or not.

430
00:34:36.065 --> 00:34:42.819
We just drank it because we didn't have nothing else to drink.

431
00:34:42.819 --> 00:34:50.135
Nobody came out to see whether this would meet standards for drinking water.

432
00:34:50.135 --> 00:34:52.949
Just dug you a hole.

433
00:34:52.949 --> 00:34:58.577
I guess like it did when you needed a toilet.

434
00:34:58.577 --> 00:35:06.456
Dig you a hole and build you a little building and sit on it.

435
00:35:06.456 --> 00:35:17.149
Nobody come out to inspect it to see where you should put it or where it should be sitting.

436
00:35:17.149 --> 00:35:19.400
That's what you used.

437
00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:31.219
Now from where I told you about us coming up to this [ . . .?] that they have there on this side Conoco gate,

438
00:35:31.219 --> 00:35:32.907
Conoco Phillips gate.

439
00:35:32.907 --> 00:35:36.847
We had people lived there but [names?].

440
00:35:36.847 --> 00:35:42.474
There was a police sitting here on the west [gate?].

441
00:35:42.474 --> 00:35:46.414
There was a . . . That was the Eglin’s.

442
00:35:46.414 --> 00:35:56.544
It was an old place where the mother and father used to live, I guess before they died.

443
00:35:56.544 --> 00:36:00.484
They were [Eglin’s?] and these people had . . .

444
00:36:00.484 --> 00:36:03.298
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you spell that?

445
00:36:03.298 --> 00:36:03.860
<v SMITH>E-G-L-I-N.

446
00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:11.177
These people had property, and they gave enough property to build a church.

447
00:36:11.177 --> 00:36:13.991
<v ARSENEAULT>The Eglin’s gave the property?

448
00:36:13.991 --> 00:36:15.679
<v SMITH>The Eglin’s, yes.

449
00:36:15.679 --> 00:36:19.056
This property to build a church.

450
00:36:19.056 --> 00:36:22.995
Then you go on down a little

451
00:36:22.995 --> 00:36:23.558
<c.p>further.

452
00:36:23.558 --> 00:36:32.000
Our family, my family, there was a house there with my aunt lived in.

453
00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:35.014
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her name?

454
00:36:35.014 --> 00:36:35.767
<v SMITH>[Scott?].

455
00:36:35.767 --> 00:36:42.548
Then next to her was the old home place.

456
00:36:42.548 --> 00:36:45.562
The old home place.

457
00:36:45.562 --> 00:36:50.836
My grandmother and grandfather who raised us.

458
00:36:50.836 --> 00:36:55.356
It was . . . This is the place.

459
00:36:55.356 --> 00:36:59.877
The big house they called it.

460
00:36:59.877 --> 00:37:03.644
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your grandparents' names?

461
00:37:03.644 --> 00:37:04.397
<v SMITH>Sullivan.

462
00:37:04.397 --> 00:37:08.918
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember their first names?

463
00:37:08.918 --> 00:37:11.178
<v SMITH>William and Loney.

464
00:37:11.178 --> 00:37:11.932
<v ARSENEAULT>L-O-N-E-Y?

465
00:37:11.932 --> 00:37:12.685
<v SMITH>Yes.

466
00:37:12.685 --> 00:37:15.699
That was her name.

467
00:37:15.699 --> 00:37:20.219
Out of this came ten children. [ . . .?]

468
00:37:20.219 --> 00:37:24.740
<v ARSENEAULT>William and Loney had ten children?

469
00:37:24.740 --> 00:37:27.000
<v SMITH>Ten children.

470
00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And your dad was one of them or your mom?

471
00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:33.000
<v SMITH>My mom.

472
00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:33.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

473
00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:36.000
What were your dad's people?

474
00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:40.000
<v SMITH>Easton, but they were further up in Westlake.

475
00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:40.500
Easton.

476
00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:41.500
<v ARSENEAULT>E-A-S- Okay.

477
00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:42.000
<v SMITH>-T-O-N.

478
00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:43.500
Eastons, and that . . .

479
00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What number was your mom of their children?

480
00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:50.885
<v SMITH>How many children she had?

481
00:37:50.885 --> 00:37:55.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Well what number was she in the ten?

482
00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:57.231
<v SMITH>Oh let's see.

483
00:37:57.231 --> 00:37:59.538
She was number four.

484
00:37:59.538 --> 00:38:08.192
And this come on back down to where I was here to this old club.

485
00:38:08.192 --> 00:38:12.808
I forgot where it was . . . called Hill Joy.

486
00:38:12.808 --> 00:38:28.385
Yes, this comes on down to where his sister and mama and this old club sitting right on . . . which one of the boys . . . Williams was their name.

487
00:38:28.385 --> 00:38:29.538
Edgar Williams.

488
00:38:29.538 --> 00:38:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Edgar Williams had a club?

489
00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:37.338
<v SMITH>Yes, he had the club.

490
00:38:37.338 --> 00:38:46.882
That stopped it right . . . That's where I stopped it, Trousdale Road.

491
00:38:46.882 --> 00:38:47.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Trousdale?

492
00:38:47.750 --> 00:38:48.618
<v SMITH>Trousdale.

493
00:38:48.618 --> 00:38:49.485
<v ARSENEAULT>T-R-O-U-S?

494
00:38:49.485 --> 00:38:50.353
<v SMITH>Yeah.

495
00:38:50.353 --> 00:38:51.221
<v ARSENEAULT>D-A-L-E?

496
00:38:51.221 --> 00:38:52.088
<v SMITH>D-A-L-E.

497
00:38:52.088 --> 00:39:08.574
Then there was just a stretch of woods and another family of people that lived past that road, Richardson’s.

498
00:39:08.574 --> 00:39:24.191
It went on back down until it took on other side of . . . What's that name . . . Would be Johnson.

499
00:39:24.191 --> 00:39:29.397
<v ARSENEAULT>Was this before World War II?

500
00:39:29.397 --> 00:39:30.265
<v SMITH>Yes.

501
00:39:30.265 --> 00:39:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

502
00:39:32.000 --> 00:39:38.443
<v SMITH>It was . . . I'm just going west on OST [Old Spanish Trail].

503
00:39:38.443 --> 00:39:39.029
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

504
00:39:39.029 --> 00:39:41.957
Through Mount Zion [Baptist Church].

505
00:39:41.957 --> 00:39:44.300
<v SMITH>Which is family lane.

506
00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:48.986
We was a good ways from Mount Zion.

507
00:39:48.986 --> 00:39:57.771
I guess the only way to do this thing is we taking in individuals here.

508
00:39:57.771 --> 00:40:00.114
I would have to . . .

509
00:40:00.114 --> 00:40:03.043
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you need another page?

510
00:40:03.043 --> 00:40:03.629
<v SMITH>Yes.

511
00:40:03.629 --> 00:40:07.143
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, this can be the west.

512
00:40:07.143 --> 00:40:13.000
We can mark east on that side I guess.

513
00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:14.625
<v SMITH>Okay.

514
00:40:14.625 --> 00:40:29.250
Right here . . . here now . . . South side of the river.

515
00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:30.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

516
00:40:30.875 --> 00:40:42.250
<v SMITH>I'm going back to the south side.

517
00:40:42.250 --> 00:40:43.875
Okay.

518
00:40:43.875 --> 00:40:47.125
Edwards, Williams.

519
00:40:47.125 --> 00:40:55.250
These are just family names.

520
00:40:55.250 --> 00:41:09.875
People who lived closest to the highway right here.

521
00:41:09.875 --> 00:41:21.250
<v ARSENEAULT>And these . . . Were these the original families?

522
00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:31.000
<v SMITH>Original families far as I know.

523
00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:35.875
Williams and Hadnots.

524
00:41:35.875 --> 00:41:37.500
<v ARSENEAULT>H-A-D-N-E . . . ?

525
00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:39.125
<v SMITH>H-A-D-N-O-T-S.

526
00:41:39.125 --> 00:41:40.750
<v ARSENEAULT>N-O-T-S.

527
00:41:40.750 --> 00:41:44.000
Rigmaiden.

528
00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:44.924
<v SMITH>Rigmaiden.

529
00:41:44.924 --> 00:41:55.087
I'm going to stop here because we come to another church.

530
00:41:55.087 --> 00:41:58.783
<v ARSENEAULT>What church was that?

531
00:41:58.783 --> 00:42:05.250
<v SMITH>I'm not coming up with a name.

532
00:42:05.250 --> 00:42:09.870
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the denomination?

533
00:42:09.870 --> 00:42:12.641
<v SMITH>Yes, it was . . .

534
00:42:12.641 --> 00:42:16.337
<v ARSENEAULT>Christ Sanctified Holy Church?

535
00:42:16.337 --> 00:42:26.500
<v SMITH>Yes, and that . . . this here . . . I should have turned this thing.

536
00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:44.978
This here after that church is right here across the road on another side would be Rigmaiden Avenue and I . . .

537
00:42:44.978 --> 00:42:45.902
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

538
00:42:45.902 --> 00:43:03.457
<v SMITH>That's here on that and you come the . . . Then when you pass Rigmaiden Avenue, there was a rice field.

539
00:43:03.457 --> 00:43:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In front of the church?

540
00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:18.227
<v SMITH>In front of the church going west.

541
00:43:18.227 --> 00:43:19.545
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

542
00:43:19.545 --> 00:43:24.818
<v SMITH>To the railroad tracks.

543
00:43:24.818 --> 00:43:31.409
<v ARSENEAULT>Who owned the rice field?

544
00:43:31.409 --> 00:43:34.045
<v SMITH>Business man.

545
00:43:34.045 --> 00:43:35.364
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

546
00:43:35.364 --> 00:43:38.000
<v SMITH>Manigin.

547
00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:41.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a white man?

548
00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:42.080
<v SMITH>Yes.

549
00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:42.760
M-A-N-A-G-I-N.

550
00:43:42.760 --> 00:43:45.480
The Managin's, right there.

551
00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:55.000
It goes from right along this west goes all the way back to north.

552
00:43:55.000 --> 00:44:05.200
Houston River was a big field but I'm only on the front line of it.

553
00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:07.240
Managin run that.

554
00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:13.360
The finally we come to this KCS track here.

555
00:44:13.360 --> 00:44:20.160
We come on down to where we're sitting here now.

556
00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:22.200
<v ARSENEAULT>The Rigmaiden Center?

557
00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:23.560
<v SMITH>Rigmaiden Center.

558
00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was here before it was built?

559
00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:36.128
<v SMITH>That old school I was telling you sit right here sometimes.

560
00:44:36.128 --> 00:44:40.663
It went on up to Prater Road.

561
00:44:40.663 --> 00:44:45.199
Next road [ . . .?] here would be Prater Road.

562
00:44:45.199 --> 00:45:02.694
Now to do further name by name I need a . . . I need something and I would need to write so I could name what houses I know.

563
00:45:02.694 --> 00:45:05.286
What people I know.

564
00:45:05.286 --> 00:45:17.597
But you come into this and I think that's pretty much where Sasol is going at that Prater Road.

565
00:45:17.597 --> 00:45:27.316
Which is on the south side of the road, but we on the north side.

566
00:45:27.316 --> 00:45:38.980
I don't know if they going to go any further than that or not from what I heard,

567
00:45:38.980 --> 00:45:49.995
and I didn't talk to anybody really other than street talks saying that know what's going on.

568
00:45:49.995 --> 00:45:57.122
Talk to somebody who say, "No, we going to do that."

569
00:45:57.122 --> 00:46:01.010
You’d have to find that out.

570
00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:27.577
How far they go down the other way because Old Spanish Trail goes all the way to Sulphur and then before you get to pass the city of Sulphur and going on out that way there was a sulfur mine there.

571
00:46:27.577 --> 00:46:36.000
But that's a little bit too much for me personally to say.

572
00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:45.887
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did . . . When you were growing up living in the old home place, where did the people work?

573
00:46:45.887 --> 00:46:48.085
Your grandparents, your parents.

574
00:46:48.085 --> 00:46:51.380
<v SMITH>They had a few sawmills around.

575
00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:51.930
<v ARSENEAULT>Sawmill?

576
00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:52.479
<v SMITH>Yes.

577
00:46:52.479 --> 00:46:57.972
The saw mill was big in here at one time.

578
00:46:57.972 --> 00:46:58.521
That's . . .

579
00:46:58.521 --> 00:47:02.915
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it close to the old home place?

580
00:47:02.915 --> 00:47:03.465
<v SMITH>Yes.

581
00:47:03.465 --> 00:47:10.606
One of them was and the other one was here where you enter.

582
00:47:10.606 --> 00:47:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the name of it?

583
00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:15.771
<v SMITH>Yes.

584
00:47:15.771 --> 00:47:18.086
<v ARSENEAULT>Conoco Phillips there?

585
00:47:18.086 --> 00:47:21.943
<v SMITH>Right across the road there.

586
00:47:21.943 --> 00:47:28.114
A man by the name of Ross Reeves.

587
00:47:28.114 --> 00:47:30.429
He was the [. . .?].

588
00:47:30.429 --> 00:47:32.743
<v ARSENEAULT>He owned it?

589
00:47:32.743 --> 00:47:37.371
<v SMITH>He didn't own the property, now.

590
00:47:37.371 --> 00:47:45.086
That's where he set his mill up at over there.

591
00:47:45.086 --> 00:47:56.657
And I'm sure at that time that's where he got some of his timber from.

592
00:47:56.657 --> 00:48:03.600
Timber was big here when I was a youngin'.

593
00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that?

594
00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:15.522
<v SMITH>Well if you worked in the summer you had a job.

595
00:48:15.522 --> 00:48:20.858
You doing what it was somebody at the sawmill.

596
00:48:20.858 --> 00:48:30.345
It was basically what was . . . Basically lumber and then they'd finish it and what have you.

597
00:48:30.345 --> 00:48:43.389
You had a good job if you worked for the saw mill but what happened during the years, they sawmilled it out.

598
00:48:43.389 --> 00:48:54.062
You can keep cutting and extracting here until you don't have no more of what you looking for.

599
00:48:54.062 --> 00:48:59.398
And this . . . There was a bigger sawmill in here.

600
00:48:59.398 --> 00:49:03.549
It was over here on [Highway] 90.

601
00:49:03.549 --> 00:49:11.257
It worked a lot of people, and it was called [Lock Morin?] Company.

602
00:49:11.257 --> 00:49:21.929
They cleared out in here to what . . . I guess the law was that you could do without destroying,

603
00:49:21.929 --> 00:49:27.858
cutting all . . . Then they left and went to Redwood, California.

604
00:49:27.858 --> 00:49:30.823
This was before my time.

605
00:49:30.823 --> 00:49:38.531
Not really before my time, but as a child I was told this.

606
00:49:38.531 --> 00:49:46.239
Some people from here went with it, and others they just played out.

607
00:49:46.239 --> 00:49:50.389
Then the saw mill just played out.

608
00:49:50.389 --> 00:50:01.655
Well the war was over and Conoco was beginning to hire men that came back here from the war.

609
00:50:01.655 --> 00:50:10.549
And Westlake and Mossville, Cities Service, all that grew right in that span of time.

610
00:50:10.549 --> 00:50:15.885
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone from your family serve in the war?

611
00:50:15.885 --> 00:50:18.850
<v SMITH>Yes, I had an uncle.

612
00:50:18.850 --> 00:50:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever talk about it?

613
00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:24.136
<v SMITH>The war?

614
00:50:24.136 --> 00:50:25.841
No, not really.

615
00:50:25.841 --> 00:50:26.977
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone . . .

616
00:50:26.977 --> 00:50:37.205
<v SMITH>Oh, not really about the war, but there's other things that happened during the war they talked about.

617
00:50:37.205 --> 00:50:38.341
<v ARSENEAULT>Like what?

618
00:50:38.341 --> 00:50:45.727
<v SMITH>Like who they were and what they did, what they got into and . . .

619
00:50:45.727 --> 00:50:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you serve?

620
00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:50.233
<v SMITH>No, I didn't go.

621
00:50:50.233 --> 00:51:03.628
To get this thing really with something to write about you'd really have to stay on Old Spanish Trail because there were those houses,

622
00:51:03.628 --> 00:51:06.419
people all in these places.

623
00:51:06.419 --> 00:51:13.674
Sometimes you look at these westerns and you think of stuff like that.

624
00:51:13.674 --> 00:51:25.953
I can relate to some of it because simply it seemed then just an old [shoe?] the people we lived back there.

625
00:51:25.953 --> 00:51:34.884
The only thing that we didn't live with the animals like they did and so forth.

626
00:51:34.884 --> 00:51:41.023
Wasn't a whole lot different because wasn't a whole lot here.

627
00:51:41.023 --> 00:51:47.721
People was not trying to get rich making a lot of money.

628
00:51:47.721 --> 00:51:57.767
I remember working the service station that's over here . . . That used to be over here on I-10 Bridge.

629
00:51:57.767 --> 00:52:00.558
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that your first job?

630
00:52:00.558 --> 00:52:07.814
<v SMITH>Oh no. My first job was in the rice fields, working in fields.

631
00:52:07.814 --> 00:52:08.930
First thing.

632
00:52:08.930 --> 00:52:15.628
That was most young people, their first thing was in the field.

633
00:52:15.628 --> 00:52:21.209
I was a pretty [ . . .?] I started working the rice fields.

634
00:52:21.209 --> 00:52:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your responsibilities?

635
00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:27.094
<v SMITH>Pulling weeds and just farm work.

636
00:52:27.094 --> 00:52:29.673
Whatever [ . . .?], didn't have no tractors.

637
00:52:29.673 --> 00:52:32.767
Know how to drive a horse.

638
00:52:32.767 --> 00:52:36.893
How to rack hay with a hay rack.

639
00:52:36.893 --> 00:52:46.692
Basically you couldn’t see us like youngins today because they don't . . . They wouldn't know what you was talking about.

640
00:52:46.692 --> 00:52:54.943
You hook up a team and they raking up because they have all this automatic stuff.

641
00:52:54.943 --> 00:53:02.164
They go and they bail hay with the way they do and so forth.

642
00:53:02.164 --> 00:53:04.742
Wouldn't even dream about it.

643
00:53:04.742 --> 00:53:06.805
Wasn't even a dream.

644
00:53:06.805 --> 00:53:11.447
You just did whatever you was told to do,

645
00:53:11.447 --> 00:53:24.855
whether it was in the field or around the boss’ house or go down and drive a herd up because of whatever reason they wanted them.

646
00:53:24.855 --> 00:53:25.887
Did that.

647
00:53:25.887 --> 00:53:30.013
That was just the work of the day.

648
00:53:30.013 --> 00:53:32.075
That's what you did.

649
00:53:32.075 --> 00:53:34.654
Wasn't much of nothing else.

650
00:53:34.654 --> 00:53:42.390
Wasn't anything else out of farm work other until these plants start coming in here.

651
00:53:42.390 --> 00:53:46.000
Putting these potted plants in here.

652
00:53:46.000 --> 00:53:50.550
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were you when you started working?

653
00:53:50.550 --> 00:53:53.394
<v SMITH>Oh, about twelve or thirteen.

654
00:53:53.394 --> 00:54:04.202
I was about twelve or thirteen and I was working for this millionaire that I tell you about, W.T.

655
00:54:04.202 --> 00:54:04.771
Burton.

656
00:54:04.771 --> 00:54:10.459
He had a [shell?] dock on the river, just south

657
00:54:10.459 --> 00:54:11.596
<c.p>of here.

658
00:54:11.596 --> 00:54:15.009
There was always lots to do.

659
00:54:15.009 --> 00:54:18.991
I think at fifty cents a day.

660
00:54:18.991 --> 00:54:21.266
<v ARSENEAULT>Fifty cents a day?

661
00:54:21.266 --> 00:54:23.541
<v SMITH>Don't sound like much.

662
00:54:23.541 --> 00:54:25.817
Pretty good money then.

663
00:54:25.817 --> 00:54:38.330
Take fifty cents and go to the dime movie and to the store, and in fact you still had some money left.

664
00:54:38.330 --> 00:54:43.450
<v ARSENEAULT>You went to the movie theater with the money?

665
00:54:43.450 --> 00:54:44.018
<v SMITH>Yes.

666
00:54:44.018 --> 00:54:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it segregated at the time?

667
00:54:48.000 --> 00:54:48.462
<v SMITH>Yes.

668
00:54:48.462 --> 00:54:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the movie theater the black people could go to?

669
00:54:54.000 --> 00:55:00.188
<v SMITH>They went to the same one the white ones went to.

670
00:55:00.188 --> 00:55:04.125
You sat upstairs and they sat downstairs.

671
00:55:04.125 --> 00:55:14.812
There was a stair that led you up and then the white people they come in they sit downstairs.

672
00:55:14.812 --> 00:55:17.062
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it in Westlake?

673
00:55:17.062 --> 00:55:18.750
<v SMITH>[Agrees] In Westlake.

674
00:55:18.750 --> 00:55:22.688
Not where what we call Westlake now.

675
00:55:22.688 --> 00:55:27.188
It was in the old part of Westlake.

676
00:55:27.188 --> 00:55:31.125
Had nothing going on there no more.

677
00:55:31.125 --> 00:55:35.062
Come right along by the railroad tracks.

678
00:55:35.062 --> 00:55:41.812
And other than that there wasn't much else for you to do.

679
00:55:41.812 --> 00:55:48.562
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were thirteen and you started working in the rice fields?

680
00:55:48.562 --> 00:55:54.750
How old were you when you worked at the service station?

681
00:55:54.750 --> 00:55:57.000
<v SMITH>About sixteen, seventeen.

682
00:55:57.000 --> 00:56:00.747
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you in school at the time?

683
00:56:00.747 --> 00:56:12.524
<v SMITH>If you had a job like that you get your work they'd let you out of school and let you go work.

684
00:56:12.524 --> 00:56:21.624
But it wasn't a whole lot to want for or to do because there was nothing there.

685
00:56:21.624 --> 00:56:22.694
Nothing there.

686
00:56:22.694 --> 00:56:25.371
Bicycle was a high priority.

687
00:56:25.371 --> 00:56:29.118
You were getting on your feet then.

688
00:56:29.118 --> 00:56:30.188
Bicycle money.

689
00:56:30.188 --> 00:56:36.612
Had a bicycle, you had a pretty good way to get around.

690
00:56:36.612 --> 00:56:58.024
This place grew after the war people that move here and came here and things began to change on how you worked and looked at life and what you expected out of it and what you got out of it.

691
00:56:58.024 --> 00:57:00.700
It made a big difference.

692
00:57:00.700 --> 00:57:13.547
A lot of people who went in the war here from staying here some of them, a lot of them, not some of them.

693
00:57:13.547 --> 00:57:24.788
A lot of them left here and went to the war after they come out they never did come back here.

694
00:57:24.788 --> 00:57:28.000
Never did come back here.

695
00:57:28.000 --> 00:57:29.681
<v ARSENEAULT>Where'd they go?

696
00:57:29.681 --> 00:57:32.482
<v SMITH>Different places where they lived.

697
00:57:32.482 --> 00:57:34.723
California and New York.

698
00:57:34.723 --> 00:57:36.963
Where did they go?

699
00:57:36.963 --> 00:57:42.005
<c.p>All of them that come out of the army.

700
00:57:42.005 --> 00:57:47.047
Some of them never came back here to live.

701
00:57:47.047 --> 00:58:00.492
They had got a taste of life at its best and they was never coming back here to live or to work the fields.

702
00:58:00.492 --> 00:58:10.016
Some families even . . . There are families from here . . . I was talking to a lady in Berkeley, California.

703
00:58:10.016 --> 00:58:11.696
She's from here.

704
00:58:11.696 --> 00:58:12.817
She's ninety.

705
00:58:12.817 --> 00:58:20.660
And we were talking about the places her grandchildren have never seen Westlake, Mossville.

706
00:58:20.660 --> 00:58:24.021
All of their roots are here.

707
00:58:24.021 --> 00:58:30.183
They never came here to live like a second class citizen.

708
00:58:30.183 --> 00:58:31.864
They never came.

709
00:58:31.864 --> 00:58:39.147
I don't know if they ever come to visit any of the people.

710
00:58:39.147 --> 00:58:46.990
Some of them did, but the ones I know they never did come back.

711
00:58:46.990 --> 00:58:48.110
Come here.

712
00:58:48.110 --> 00:59:04.916
Now, some of them in this first buyout that Condea Vista did some of them come back here because they were heirs to property and got that money and stuff.

713
00:59:04.916 --> 00:59:11.079
But they never did come back no more and stay here.

714
00:59:11.079 --> 00:59:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did the first buyout start?

715
00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:21.287
<v SMITH>Let's see, '71 . . . About '70 . . . This is not accurate.

716
00:59:21.287 --> 00:59:23.382
About '77, '78.

717
00:59:23.382 --> 00:59:25.478
First buyout started.

718
00:59:25.478 --> 00:59:36.654
First they had a . . . Had this thing about the drinking water that brought in the problem,

719
00:59:36.654 --> 00:59:42.243
and I been drinking it all my life.

720
00:59:42.243 --> 00:59:43.640
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened?

721
00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:49.926
<v SMITH>Well they found out it wasn't decent to drink.

722
00:59:49.926 --> 00:59:58.309
Other people had had . . . I didn't have any that I know of.

723
00:59:58.309 --> 01:00:04.596
Other people had medical problems about the drinking water.

724
01:00:04.596 --> 01:00:11.581
This water distribution place is right behind this building here.

725
01:00:11.581 --> 01:00:20.662
It was good for years, but now all of a sudden it wasn't.

726
01:00:20.662 --> 01:00:26.949
I'm glad I didn't get any contamination or anything.

727
01:00:26.949 --> 01:00:29.044
I don't remember.

728
01:00:29.044 --> 01:00:30.441
But they

729
01:00:30.441 --> 01:00:33.235
<c.p>had to stop it.

730
01:00:33.235 --> 01:00:37.426
Run the water from somewhere else.

731
01:00:37.426 --> 01:00:46.507
I don't remember, but that was the start of this whole buyout situation.

732
01:00:46.507 --> 01:00:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How'd it get contaminated?

733
01:00:50.000 --> 01:00:52.898
<v SMITH>They were pumping excess waste.

734
01:00:52.898 --> 01:00:54.058
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was?

735
01:00:54.058 --> 01:00:55.796
<v SMITH>Conoco and Vista.

736
01:00:55.796 --> 01:01:06.230
Into the ground and it was still using this . . . This is as much as I know about it.

737
01:01:06.230 --> 01:01:16.664
I don't know a whole lot about it, how it start, but I think that's how it started.

738
01:01:16.664 --> 01:01:31.735
And somebody got sick and it started this thing and find out that the water you’re drinking . . . And that's where all this started from buying out.

739
01:01:31.735 --> 01:01:34.053
Never would have been.

740
01:01:34.053 --> 01:01:43.327
I don't think we'd ever come to this selling place had not been for that situation.

741
01:01:43.327 --> 01:01:47.965
And then they began to have other problems.

742
01:01:47.965 --> 01:01:54.341
This releasing of stuff . . . this releasing of stuff in the air.

743
01:01:54.341 --> 01:01:57.239
People claim sickness from it.

744
01:01:57.239 --> 01:02:01.296
And I say claim, might have been.

745
01:02:01.296 --> 01:02:03.035
I don't know.

746
01:02:03.035 --> 01:02:07.673
I don't know one . . . I didn't have it.

747
01:02:07.673 --> 01:02:11.730
I didn't have the problem with it.

748
01:02:11.730 --> 01:02:25.062
It was enough to come to where we are here now with this thing as to make the decision of closing this down.

749
01:02:25.062 --> 01:02:28.540
This living quarters for Mossville people.

750
01:02:28.540 --> 01:02:34.916
It must have been strong evidence of what they were doing.

751
01:02:34.916 --> 01:02:51.726
They wouldn't just went out spent that kind of money for nothing just to . . . That's about it for what we're looking for here as the people living and moving,

752
01:02:51.726 --> 01:02:56.942
where they going and who was . . . What was Mossville.

753
01:02:56.942 --> 01:03:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why did your family move here?

754
01:03:01.000 --> 01:03:07.861
<v SMITH>As far as I know this is where they’ve always been.

755
01:03:07.861 --> 01:03:11.603
Like my grandparents and my mother.

756
01:03:11.603 --> 01:03:13.474
These people that [ . . .?].

757
01:03:13.474 --> 01:03:19.088
That's . . . I don't know anywhere else they come from.

758
01:03:19.088 --> 01:03:20.335
<v ARSENEAULT>Both sides?

759
01:03:20.335 --> 01:03:25.948
<v SMITH>I don't know that much about my daddy's people.

760
01:03:25.948 --> 01:03:30.314
I know they were Westlake, Mossville people.

761
01:03:30.314 --> 01:03:41.541
Other than that I don't . . . My daddy wasn't a close part of my family to really know him.

762
01:03:41.541 --> 01:03:47.155
Where he come from other than he's from here.

763
01:03:47.155 --> 01:03:50.273
That's what I know about.

764
01:03:50.273 --> 01:03:55.263
But now my mother's folks I follow them.

765
01:03:55.263 --> 01:04:02.124
They came from like . . . Can't think of the name right now.

766
01:04:02.124 --> 01:04:07.737
Sometimes when you get this age you don't think.

767
01:04:07.737 --> 01:04:10.232
Don't write it down.

768
01:04:10.232 --> 01:04:15.845
They were like from Lake Charles . . . out and about.

769
01:04:15.845 --> 01:04:23.954
Lake Charles and I think from what I was told this man started.

770
01:04:23.954 --> 01:04:42.665
My grandfather think all you had to do was homestead it and worked here so much don't starve and that was your property if you paid the tax on it.

771
01:04:42.665 --> 01:04:46.407
That's what I know about it.

772
01:04:46.407 --> 01:04:50.773
So we migrated right here in Mossville.

773
01:04:50.773 --> 01:04:55.139
All the family here in Mossville, Westlake.

774
01:04:55.139 --> 01:05:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How many children in your family did your mom have?

775
01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:03.303
<v SMITH>Just me.

776
01:05:03.303 --> 01:05:05.909
<v ARSENEAULT>You're the only one?

777
01:05:05.909 --> 01:05:06.561
<v SMITH>Yes.

778
01:05:06.561 --> 01:05:07.864
Just me.

779
01:05:07.864 --> 01:05:16.333
I don't know, if you think of anything else you can call me.

780
01:05:16.333 --> 01:05:16.985
You

781
01:05:16.985 --> 01:05:18.939
<c.p>have my number.

782
01:05:18.939 --> 01:05:21.545
See if I remember.

783
01:05:21.545 --> 01:05:29.364
<v ARSENEAULT>We've got a couple more questions if you don't mind me asking.

784
01:05:29.364 --> 01:05:34.576
So you already told me who lived nearby.

785
01:05:34.576 --> 01:05:41.742
So when you were growing up who were the community leaders?

786
01:05:41.742 --> 01:05:42.394
<v SMITH>Rigmaidens.

787
01:05:42.394 --> 01:05:43.045
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

788
01:05:43.045 --> 01:05:45.000
Jacob Rigmaiden?

789
01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:45.842
<v SMITH>Yes.

790
01:05:45.842 --> 01:05:51.737
Jacob, Josh, and that was basically it.

791
01:05:51.737 --> 01:05:58.474
They did what was needed for the community.

792
01:05:58.474 --> 01:06:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like what?

793
01:06:01.000 --> 01:06:05.310
<v SMITH>Like really nothing because that's why they were the leaders.

794
01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:13.929
They didn't know nothing and all they wanted to be doing is, "Who is this coming in the court house?"

795
01:06:13.929 --> 01:06:15.222
"Oh that's Jake [ . . .?]."

796
01:06:15.222 --> 01:06:16.946
"What did he do?"

797
01:06:16.946 --> 01:06:17.377
"Nothing.

798
01:06:17.377 --> 01:06:19.100
Look at the place.

799
01:06:19.100 --> 01:06:19.531
Nothing.

800
01:06:19.531 --> 01:06:20.824
Not a thing."

801
01:06:20.824 --> 01:06:24.703
It's got his name on these signs around here.

802
01:06:24.703 --> 01:06:26.858
Jacob Rigmaiden and not nothing.

803
01:06:26.858 --> 01:06:32.029
There's nothing that nobody can tell you that they did worth anything.

804
01:06:32.029 --> 01:06:33.753
For the school, anything.

805
01:06:33.753 --> 01:06:40.218
It was just that if there was a minor problem or something who they come?

806
01:06:40.218 --> 01:06:43.234
They come find Jake and Josh Rigmaiden.

807
01:06:43.234 --> 01:06:45.820
And I'm not putting it down.

808
01:06:45.820 --> 01:06:50.992
You can't something when you don't have something to do something with.

809
01:06:50.992 --> 01:06:54.439
If you don't know nothing, can't do nothing.

810
01:06:54.439 --> 01:06:56.163
You don't know nothing.

811
01:06:56.163 --> 01:06:57.025
It's not

812
01:06:57.025 --> 01:07:03.059
<c.p>a shameful thing, but it's just like you driving a vehicle without a license.

813
01:07:03.059 --> 01:07:04.782
It's against the law.

814
01:07:04.782 --> 01:07:06.937
You never come to nothing.

815
01:07:06.937 --> 01:07:08.230
That's simply it.

816
01:07:08.230 --> 01:07:15.126
They were just here because they were who they were in the community at the time.

817
01:07:15.126 --> 01:07:21.590
His daddy was a preacher and his mama was . . . Stuff like that kind of stuff.

818
01:07:21.590 --> 01:07:25.038
That don't get you nowhere in the world.

819
01:07:25.038 --> 01:07:27.623
You don't [ . . .?] behind nothing like that.

820
01:07:27.623 --> 01:07:39.690
Got to know something to go before somebody and then they . . . Back then Reed was here he was the sheriff as long as I can remember coming up.

821
01:07:39.690 --> 01:07:42.276
<v ARSENEAULT>He was the sheriff of Mossville?

822
01:07:42.276 --> 01:07:43.138
<v SMITH>Calcasieu Parish.

823
01:07:43.138 --> 01:07:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

824
01:07:44.000 --> 01:07:44.882
<v SMITH>Ham Reed.

825
01:07:44.882 --> 01:07:47.088
His daddy was the sheriff.

826
01:07:47.088 --> 01:07:50.618
Now I go that far back [to remember?].

827
01:07:50.618 --> 01:07:53.265
But it was who you were.

828
01:07:53.265 --> 01:07:55.912
This thing get out of hand.

829
01:07:55.912 --> 01:08:07.382
When your child breaks the law and didn't do nothing about it and his child breaks the law and they do something about it, that's bad.

830
01:08:07.382 --> 01:08:08.265
That's bad.

831
01:08:08.265 --> 01:08:14.000
It's leading to something down the road that's going to come to . . .

832
01:08:14.000 --> 01:08:15.456
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you mean favoritism?

833
01:08:15.456 --> 01:08:16.913
They were showing favoritism?

834
01:08:16.913 --> 01:08:18.005
<v SMITH>Well like that.

835
01:08:18.005 --> 01:08:27.836
But if your child was . . . got into something out there and they locked him up my child was right there with him they didn't lock him up.

836
01:08:27.836 --> 01:08:35.482
It makes a bad situation on that individual as to who you'll be in coming because he's looking for somebody to

837
01:08:35.482 --> 01:08:36.938
<c.p>always walk before him.

838
01:08:36.938 --> 01:08:40.215
"Oh my dad will get me out of this.

839
01:08:40.215 --> 01:08:42.036
He knows the high sheriff."

840
01:08:42.036 --> 01:08:44.949
That was all they was doing out there.

841
01:08:44.949 --> 01:08:53.687
They'd run out there these cars and things during election time after the voting time come get these black folks to vote for him.

842
01:08:53.687 --> 01:08:55.872
That was all . . . It was nothing.

843
01:08:55.872 --> 01:08:59.877
A lot of them didn't even deserve to be in there.

844
01:08:59.877 --> 01:09:01.697
But that's what it was.

845
01:09:01.697 --> 01:09:10.800
That's why, because whatever I need Mr. Reed will give it to me if I ask for it and that kind of stuff like that.

846
01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:15.169
<v ARSENEAULT>So the politicians were using the local leaders to get the votes.

847
01:09:15.169 --> 01:09:15.897
<v SMITH>Oh yes.

848
01:09:15.897 --> 01:09:16.990
Politician used that.

849
01:09:16.990 --> 01:09:23.908
They used to come out there and their cars and pick you up and take you to the polls.

850
01:09:23.908 --> 01:09:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who did?

851
01:09:25.000 --> 01:09:26.994
<v SMITH>Politicians before they start the . . .

852
01:09:26.994 --> 01:09:28.590
<v ARSENEAULT>Remember anybody in particular?

853
01:09:28.590 --> 01:09:33.376
<v SMITH>No. I know some elections like these that I'm telling you about.

854
01:09:33.376 --> 01:09:33.775
Sheriff.

855
01:09:33.775 --> 01:09:34.573
Just whoever.

856
01:09:34.573 --> 01:09:36.169
It was a thing.

857
01:09:36.169 --> 01:09:44.944
They'd get . . . They'd come out here in the community and they'd give these black guys money and fill their car with gas,

858
01:09:44.944 --> 01:09:52.124
and all you had to do was ride around and see who wanted to go to the polls.

859
01:09:52.124 --> 01:09:54.118
Take them to the polls.

860
01:09:54.118 --> 01:10:00.500
I don't know if there's a law against that or not but that's really what happened.

861
01:10:00.500 --> 01:10:04.489
And a lot of these guys, they wasn't worth it.

862
01:10:04.489 --> 01:10:08.079
They were just using people, using them for that.

863
01:10:08.079 --> 01:10:13.663
I can see a politician now wanting to get elected as far as doing

864
01:10:13.663 --> 01:10:22.039
<c.p>some things like that if the person calls you up and asks you and you're able to furnish transportation for them.

865
01:10:22.039 --> 01:10:28.022
But just to go around . . . And half of them couldn’t read and write no way.

866
01:10:28.022 --> 01:10:31.213
They didn't know who they was voting for.

867
01:10:31.213 --> 01:10:34.404
Voting for who they told to vote for.

868
01:10:34.404 --> 01:10:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who told them?

869
01:10:36.000 --> 01:10:37.061
<v SMITH>The politicians.

870
01:10:37.061 --> 01:10:38.121
The politicians.

871
01:10:38.121 --> 01:10:41.833
Who they were supposed to be backing.

872
01:10:41.833 --> 01:10:45.015
Make no difference who it was.

873
01:10:45.015 --> 01:10:47.136
They all did it.

874
01:10:47.136 --> 01:10:49.258
It was all wrong.

875
01:10:49.258 --> 01:10:56.682
I mean the High Sheriff, senator, legislator, whoever was on . . . whoever was on there.

876
01:10:56.682 --> 01:10:59.333
That goes way, way deep.

877
01:10:59.333 --> 01:11:02.515
People didn't know how to vote.

878
01:11:02.515 --> 01:11:07.288
A lot of them couldn't hardly read or write.

879
01:11:07.288 --> 01:11:10.470
They didn't know how to vote.

880
01:11:10.470 --> 01:11:17.364
They didn't know nothing about diplomatic situations other than what they were told.

881
01:11:17.364 --> 01:11:23.727
"Now you vote here because . . ." They didn't know a thing about politics.

882
01:11:23.727 --> 01:11:25.848
Where'd they learn it?

883
01:11:25.848 --> 01:11:27.970
They didn't know any.

884
01:11:27.970 --> 01:11:31.152
Learn from who told them that.

885
01:11:31.152 --> 01:11:40.697
Who did the most for them when they was in need or when their job was in trouble.

886
01:11:40.697 --> 01:11:49.182
When trouble come in any situation where they need some help and know where to go.

887
01:11:49.182 --> 01:11:51.303
That was always important.

888
01:11:51.303 --> 01:11:56.076
It's that way now just a little more educated.

889
01:11:56.076 --> 01:11:58.727
It's not all that different.

890
01:11:58.727 --> 01:12:06.152
It's just some people are a little more educated as to what they want.

891
01:12:06.152 --> 01:12:10.394
They want a little more than someone else.

892
01:12:10.394 --> 01:12:16.227
So this thing from where I stand come a long ways.

893
01:12:16.227 --> 01:12:19.409
I walked from here to school.

894
01:12:19.409 --> 01:12:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>To Westlake?

895
01:12:21.000 --> 01:12:22.115
<v SMITH>To Westlake.

896
01:12:22.115 --> 01:12:31.033
When a bunch of little white children pass right by us and threw eggs at us.

897
01:12:31.033 --> 01:12:33.820
Couldn't get on that bus.

898
01:12:33.820 --> 01:12:36.049
<v ARSENEAULT>You had to walk?

899
01:12:36.049 --> 01:12:37.164
<v SMITH>Well sure.

900
01:12:37.164 --> 01:12:39.393
It wasn't for us.

901
01:12:39.393 --> 01:12:44.967
You wasn't getting on that bus with them white children.

902
01:12:44.967 --> 01:12:46.639
Wouldn't stand it.

903
01:12:46.639 --> 01:12:53.328
So this like many thing . . . See in matters like this subject matter,

904
01:12:53.328 --> 01:13:03.361
who would be able to stand up and talk you couldn't get many black people who'd say anything,

905
01:13:03.361 --> 01:13:08.377
simply because of subject matter, what's subject to happen.

906
01:13:08.377 --> 01:13:14.508
Who’s running and how much money is put into this thing.

907
01:13:14.508 --> 01:13:17.295
Putting into somebody else's pocket.

908
01:13:17.295 --> 01:13:23.426
It's a lot better, but it ain't a whole lot different.

909
01:13:23.426 --> 01:13:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How far did you have to walk for school?

910
01:13:29.000 --> 01:13:29.698
<v SMITH>Okay.

911
01:13:29.698 --> 01:13:31.792
This is Rigmaiden.

912
01:13:31.792 --> 01:13:34.585
There is [New Field?].

913
01:13:34.585 --> 01:13:38.075
Just past the Trousdale Road.

914
01:13:38.075 --> 01:13:41.566
<v ARSENEAULT>How many miles was it?

915
01:13:41.566 --> 01:13:45.057
<v SMITH>About four or five miles.

916
01:13:45.057 --> 01:13:50.642
To [Gillary?] Street where it come to that.

917
01:13:50.642 --> 01:13:59.019
They got a . . . I believe that little school now is a church.

918
01:13:59.019 --> 01:14:06.000
Somebody bought and made a church out of it.

919
01:14:06.000 --> 01:14:07.252
<v ARSENEAULT>All black?

920
01:14:07.252 --> 01:14:08.504
<v SMITH>All black.

921
01:14:08.504 --> 01:14:11.635
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any teachers?

922
01:14:11.635 --> 01:14:12.887
<v SMITH>Oh yes.

923
01:14:12.887 --> 01:14:19.148
Ms. Hayes, [Luanna?] Hayes was the principal at one time.

924
01:14:19.148 --> 01:14:23.530
Scott was the principal at one time.

925
01:14:23.530 --> 01:14:25.409
Maybe Ben Art.

926
01:14:25.409 --> 01:14:27.913
Ms. Green, oh yes.

927
01:14:27.913 --> 01:14:31.043
I still remember the teachers.

928
01:14:31.043 --> 01:14:34.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have any favorite teachers?

929
01:14:34.800 --> 01:14:36.052
<v SMITH>Did I?

930
01:14:36.052 --> 01:14:41.687
No. I didn't get along well with the teachers.

931
01:14:41.687 --> 01:14:44.191
I was too smart.

932
01:14:44.191 --> 01:14:55.461
I was always getting sent home for something I didn't . . . thought that I could say or didn't say.

933
01:14:55.461 --> 01:14:59.843
I didn't get very far with that.

934
01:14:59.843 --> 01:15:12.991
Then finally in '50 I got a chance to get away from here so I wasn't worried with that no more.

935
01:15:12.991 --> 01:15:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You had a chance to get away?

936
01:15:18.000 --> 01:15:27.758
<v SMITH>Yes, I left here and went out West out to California.

937
01:15:27.758 --> 01:15:31.306
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were you?

938
01:15:31.306 --> 01:15:32.194
<v SMITH>Seventeen.

939
01:15:32.194 --> 01:15:38.403
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did you live in California?

940
01:15:38.403 --> 01:15:49.935
<v SMITH>Between California and Houston [Texas] the rest of my life, and still now.

941
01:15:49.935 --> 01:15:50.823
I

942
01:15:50.823 --> 01:16:05.903
<c.p>finally . . . My daddy died come back here, and then after mother died I just stayed in Houston.

943
01:16:05.903 --> 01:16:13.000
I was doing all right in Houston.

944
01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:21.630
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your . . . When you think back to Mossville what's the earliest memory that you have?

945
01:16:21.630 --> 01:16:22.709
<v SMITH>About Mossville?

946
01:16:22.709 --> 01:16:33.497
It wasn't a person . . . wasn't a place that was decent enough to bring up and the way they did it.

947
01:16:33.497 --> 01:16:42.667
Any person that you try to educate or bring up in life had nothing to show you.

948
01:16:42.667 --> 01:16:44.824
Nothing to tell you.

949
01:16:44.824 --> 01:16:46.982
No future for you.

950
01:16:46.982 --> 01:16:48.061
No vision.

951
01:16:48.061 --> 01:16:48.600
Nothing.

952
01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:49.139
Nothing.

953
01:16:49.139 --> 01:16:51.297
Nothing look like here.

954
01:16:51.297 --> 01:16:56.691
Look like if you had focused on something then maybe.

955
01:16:56.691 --> 01:17:02.085
Look like they do everything they could to hide that.

956
01:17:02.085 --> 01:17:04.242
You couldn't do that.

957
01:17:04.242 --> 01:17:05.321
No way.

958
01:17:05.321 --> 01:17:09.636
There was no help here for school children.

959
01:17:09.636 --> 01:17:13.952
There was no encouragement here for school children.

960
01:17:13.952 --> 01:17:17.188
No encouragement here for school children.

961
01:17:17.188 --> 01:17:26.358
No help if you looked at one thing, "Boy, when I get out of here I'm leaving."

962
01:17:26.358 --> 01:17:30.673
That's the only thing that was up here.

963
01:17:30.673 --> 01:17:36.067
Get away from here when you get out of here.

964
01:17:36.067 --> 01:17:38.224
Get away from here.

965
01:17:38.224 --> 01:17:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You knew there was something better?

966
01:17:42.000 --> 01:17:43.108
<v SMITH>Oh yes.

967
01:17:43.108 --> 01:17:44.215
Better things.

968
01:17:44.215 --> 01:17:50.862
I knew that this wasn't life the way it's supposed to be.

969
01:17:50.862 --> 01:17:58.615
Not according to things I saw and read and people that living way better.

970
01:17:58.615 --> 01:18:02.492
There's nothing to do, I know that.

971
01:18:02.492 --> 01:18:09.138
There's always something better if you apply yourself and look for it.

972
01:18:09.138 --> 01:18:20.769
But as long as somebody else is directing your path, telling you what to do look and see what they're doing.

973
01:18:20.769 --> 01:18:24.646
What are you telling me this for?

974
01:18:24.646 --> 01:18:26.862
What have you got?

975
01:18:26.862 --> 01:18:30.185
And that's basically all you got

976
01:18:30.185 --> 01:18:30.738
<c.p>there.

977
01:18:30.738 --> 01:18:35.169
Somebody can tell you there’s always something better.

978
01:18:35.169 --> 01:18:37.938
Didn't even go to school.

979
01:18:37.938 --> 01:18:41.262
Tell you how to do things.

980
01:18:41.262 --> 01:18:45.692
That's really the funny part of this thing.

981
01:18:45.692 --> 01:18:49.569
Where did you go to school at?

982
01:18:49.569 --> 01:18:50.123
Nowhere.

983
01:18:50.123 --> 01:18:57.877
And people that had something for me, they always kept it hid from me.

984
01:18:57.877 --> 01:19:08.400
These people use that educate us to educate us, them people not more educated than somebody walking the street.

985
01:19:08.400 --> 01:19:18.369
Them people . . . They say common knowledge and knew how to do things like white folks wanted it done.

986
01:19:18.369 --> 01:19:20.031
And that worked.

987
01:19:20.031 --> 01:19:22.246
That worked back then.

988
01:19:22.246 --> 01:19:27.785
Don't worry about that just do like I tell you.

989
01:19:27.785 --> 01:19:30.000
That was it.

990
01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the protests during civil rights?

991
01:19:35.000 --> 01:19:37.000
<v SMITH>I wasn't into that.

992
01:19:37.000 --> 01:19:39.000
I wasn't into that.

993
01:19:39.000 --> 01:19:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were you?

994
01:19:41.000 --> 01:19:43.323
<v SMITH>Would I think about it?

995
01:19:43.323 --> 01:19:47.969
No. I was partly in Texas and partly in California.

996
01:19:47.969 --> 01:19:49.827
I wasn't into that.

997
01:19:49.827 --> 01:19:58.655
I wasn't into . . . There's a bunch of leaders that we had that was leading you down the wrong way.

998
01:19:58.655 --> 01:20:13.987
They looking for strength in their own what they wanted and on somebody's payroll doing like what they said do it and telling you . . . You didn't know if you was right or wrong.

999
01:20:13.987 --> 01:20:15.381
How'd you know?

1000
01:20:15.381 --> 01:20:21.420
You ain't educated enough to know what he's bringing you that's the truth.

1001
01:20:21.420 --> 01:20:25.137
Some things you pick up in the paper.

1002
01:20:25.137 --> 01:20:26.996
You don't know [him?].

1003
01:20:26.996 --> 01:20:32.106
See slavery, slavery didn't start with the [edging?] of white folks.

1004
01:20:32.106 --> 01:20:33.965
Negros brought slavery on.

1005
01:20:33.965 --> 01:20:36.288
They sold their own slaves.

1006
01:20:36.288 --> 01:20:40.469
The white people, how you think they got that?

1007
01:20:40.469 --> 01:20:50.690
They didn't just come out and take you and stick you in a boat and send you to Mississippi to pick cotton.

1008
01:20:50.690 --> 01:20:52.549
That was originated from

1009
01:20:52.549 --> 01:20:53.013
<c.p>somewhere.

1010
01:20:53.013 --> 01:20:53.942
That's homegrown.

1011
01:20:53.942 --> 01:20:54.407
Inside.

1012
01:20:54.407 --> 01:20:54.872
Inside.

1013
01:20:54.872 --> 01:21:02.305
When you get the fact that you know what they doing inside you can get somewhere.

1014
01:21:02.305 --> 01:21:08.345
Get away from here and that's the main thing I want to do.

1015
01:21:08.345 --> 01:21:09.739
Not just here.

1016
01:21:09.739 --> 01:21:13.456
California some places no different than nowhere else.

1017
01:21:13.456 --> 01:21:14.385
Sometimes worse.

1018
01:21:14.385 --> 01:21:19.960
But it's who you know that's doing what they should be doing.

1019
01:21:19.960 --> 01:21:23.212
That fellow's daddy was a senator here.

1020
01:21:23.212 --> 01:21:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Whose dad was a senator?

1021
01:21:26.000 --> 01:21:31.928
<v SMITH>No, I'm just talking about . . . So his son's going to take his seat.

1022
01:21:31.928 --> 01:21:32.840
For what?

1023
01:21:32.840 --> 01:21:34.664
What'd your daddy do?

1024
01:21:34.664 --> 01:21:35.121
Nothing.

1025
01:21:35.121 --> 01:21:37.401
Not even to help you.

1026
01:21:37.401 --> 01:21:41.505
Couldn't see you’re a prisoner in your own home.

1027
01:21:41.505 --> 01:21:46.065
Such thing as being a prisoner in your own home.

1028
01:21:46.065 --> 01:21:49.257
That's the way some of them was.

1029
01:21:49.257 --> 01:21:56.098
They daddy was able to help them in situations but when you sellout to slavery.

1030
01:21:56.098 --> 01:21:57.922
That's the bad part.

1031
01:21:57.922 --> 01:22:00.202
You never get no money.

1032
01:22:00.202 --> 01:22:05.674
Always be trying to climb a tree with no limbs on it.

1033
01:22:05.674 --> 01:22:08.410
That's a hard thing to do.

1034
01:22:08.410 --> 01:22:09.779
I tried it.

1035
01:22:09.779 --> 01:22:10.691
I know.

1036
01:22:10.691 --> 01:22:12.515
So I don't know.

1037
01:22:12.515 --> 01:22:20.267
I have some personal things about slavery that I don't hold white folks [fault?] slavery it is.

1038
01:22:20.267 --> 01:22:24.827
Some of the people put your own self in slavery.

1039
01:22:24.827 --> 01:22:29.388
What'd you do to try and do anything about it?

1040
01:22:29.388 --> 01:22:32.580
That's the whole situation of this thing.

1041
01:22:32.580 --> 01:22:34.404
That's the situation here.

1042
01:22:34.404 --> 01:22:42.612
People that's working around here, they don't even qualify to run a recreation center, but they in it.

1043
01:22:42.612 --> 01:22:45.349
That's the most education they got.

1044
01:22:45.349 --> 01:22:49.909
Where did you . . . Where did you get your movement from?

1045
01:22:49.909 --> 01:22:54.469
Where did you get what you supposed to do here?

1046
01:22:54.469 --> 01:23:06.326
No. Only thing they doing is treating one or two people right like they call right because Rigmaiden was a . . . Rigmaiden was a name in Mossville.

1047
01:23:06.326 --> 01:23:07.238
For what?

1048
01:23:07.238 --> 01:23:09.062
What did he do?

1049
01:23:09.062 --> 01:23:14.078
They tell me that never one of them . . . What'd he do?

1050
01:23:14.078 --> 01:23:14.990
He done [ . . .?].

1051
01:23:14.990 --> 01:23:24.111
Went up there and they put the building up here to keep them from going to school with white folks.

1052
01:23:24.111 --> 01:23:26.391
He did nothing for me.

1053
01:23:26.391 --> 01:23:31.407
Wouldn't have cost near as much to educate a room full

1054
01:23:31.407 --> 01:23:35.511
<c.p>of people if they'd have done the right thing.

1055
01:23:35.511 --> 01:23:37.792
What'd you need this for?

1056
01:23:37.792 --> 01:23:42.352
You find one of these just about on every corner.

1057
01:23:42.352 --> 01:23:46.000
You go to Westlake you'll find one.

1058
01:23:46.000 --> 01:23:47.393
What are they doing?

1059
01:23:47.393 --> 01:23:49.830
I sat there and look at them.

1060
01:23:49.830 --> 01:23:51.571
Not one thing they do.

1061
01:23:51.571 --> 01:23:52.964
They cut the grass.

1062
01:23:52.964 --> 01:23:56.446
Man they run that mower every day all day long.

1063
01:23:56.446 --> 01:23:59.929
I sit there at that park and look at them.

1064
01:23:59.929 --> 01:24:01.321
That's what they do.

1065
01:24:01.321 --> 01:24:02.714
What did they do?

1066
01:24:02.714 --> 01:24:05.848
Gave two or three people a pretty good job.

1067
01:24:05.848 --> 01:24:11.768
They don't know nothing and don't have to do nothing because they don't know nothing to do.

1068
01:24:11.768 --> 01:24:16.991
But what'd he come back and bring to them, say, "We going to change this."

1069
01:24:16.991 --> 01:24:17.339
Okay [ . . .?].

1070
01:24:17.339 --> 01:24:18.732
Hurting your own self.

1071
01:24:18.732 --> 01:24:19.429
That's it.

1072
01:24:19.429 --> 01:24:21.170
Well you got anything else?

1073
01:24:21.170 --> 01:24:22.214
That's about it.

1074
01:24:22.214 --> 01:24:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any crime in Mossville?

1075
01:24:25.000 --> 01:24:25.545
<v SMITH>Crime?

1076
01:24:25.545 --> 01:24:27.182
No, not personally.

1077
01:24:27.182 --> 01:24:28.818
Not talked about.

1078
01:24:28.818 --> 01:24:30.455
Somebody told me.

1079
01:24:30.455 --> 01:24:32.636
I don't really know.

1080
01:24:32.636 --> 01:24:37.000
They wasn't doing crime when I was here.

1081
01:24:37.000 --> 01:24:39.182
They . . . Nothing but dope.

1082
01:24:39.182 --> 01:24:44.636
I see that and read it and hear about it.

1083
01:24:44.636 --> 01:24:55.000
You hear about that everywhere, but nothing that I can really point out that would make sense of anything.

1084
01:24:55.000 --> 01:25:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They mentioned a murder in like 1950s, an unsolved . . . Was it an unsolved murder or something like that in the 1950s?

1085
01:25:07.000 --> 01:25:13.273
<v SMITH>I don't think I . . . I wasn't here in the 1950s.

1086
01:25:13.273 --> 01:25:17.037
Of course I know there's crime.

1087
01:25:17.037 --> 01:25:20.174
I'm not . . . There's crime everywhere.

1088
01:25:20.174 --> 01:25:30.839
But it has nothing to do with you not having what it takes to educate a mind.

1089
01:25:30.839 --> 01:25:38.366
See there's some children you can't educate because he don't want it.

1090
01:25:38.366 --> 01:25:42.758
He wants to be like street people.

1091
01:25:42.758 --> 01:25:45.267
What do they do?

1092
01:25:45.267 --> 01:25:49.658
He wants to be like the criminals.

1093
01:25:49.658 --> 01:25:50.913
Doesn’t want

1094
01:25:50.913 --> 01:25:51.540
<c.p>it.

1095
01:25:51.540 --> 01:26:04.087
They wants to be like somebody that's walking around doing nothing and claim to be somebody—and what have you done?

1096
01:26:04.087 --> 01:26:06.596
What have you done?

1097
01:26:06.596 --> 01:26:07.224
Nothing.

1098
01:26:07.224 --> 01:26:17.888
I don't know any crime here is important enough for me to remember what the outcome was.

1099
01:26:17.888 --> 01:26:19.770
Didn’t benefit anything.

1100
01:26:19.770 --> 01:26:21.652
I don't know.

1101
01:26:21.652 --> 01:26:26.671
I didn't grow up in dope and alcohol.

1102
01:26:26.671 --> 01:26:30.435
I didn't grow up in that.

1103
01:26:30.435 --> 01:26:34.826
<v ARSENEAULT>Well I have a lot of questions.

1104
01:26:34.826 --> 01:26:36.081
<v SMITH>Go ahead.

1105
01:26:36.081 --> 01:26:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Are you . . . because we're at an hour and a half I don't know if you're tired or not.

1106
01:26:48.000 --> 01:26:49.753
<v SMITH>No, I'm alright.

1107
01:26:49.753 --> 01:26:50.337
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

1108
01:26:50.337 --> 01:27:01.438
I'm curious about when you grew up did you grow up on the old home place with your grandparents?

1109
01:27:01.438 --> 01:27:06.697
Is that where you spent most of your time?

1110
01:27:06.697 --> 01:27:07.281
<v SMITH>[Agrees]

1111
01:27:07.281 --> 01:27:11.371
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did they grow?

1112
01:27:11.371 --> 01:27:15.461
<v SMITH>They grew greens, tomatoes, okra, potatoes, onions.

1113
01:27:15.461 --> 01:27:18.382
They grow lots of stuff.

1114
01:27:18.382 --> 01:27:22.472
That's one of my [objectives?] right there.

1115
01:27:22.472 --> 01:27:28.315
They grow lots of stuff but they didn't have sense

1116
01:27:28.315 --> 01:27:36.494
<c.p>enough to market it and get the revenue when it could benefit somebody else.

1117
01:27:36.494 --> 01:27:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>There was no truck farming?

1118
01:27:40.000 --> 01:27:41.813
<v SMITH>No, you missed me.

1119
01:27:41.813 --> 01:27:57.222
I'm talking about what they grow they take down to the general store to trade for what I can get out of you what you can get out of me and see who's ahead.

1120
01:27:57.222 --> 01:28:01.754
Say it's good enough for them if they do that.

1121
01:28:01.754 --> 01:28:03.567
Didn't have truck pastures.

1122
01:28:03.567 --> 01:28:14.443
Had enough to when they go to the store and make a trade and do this and feed . . . As long as everything was fed.

1123
01:28:14.443 --> 01:28:25.773
As long as the hogs was fed, and the cattle had something to eat, chickens had some scratch and we have what to cook for.

1124
01:28:25.773 --> 01:28:27.586
That's all that mattered.

1125
01:28:27.586 --> 01:28:28.946
That's not life.

1126
01:28:28.946 --> 01:28:31.665
That's not life for human beings.

1127
01:28:31.665 --> 01:28:34.837
Life got to be more than that.

1128
01:28:34.837 --> 01:28:36.650
That could quit anytime.

1129
01:28:36.650 --> 01:28:38.010
That's not life.

1130
01:28:38.010 --> 01:28:43.448
Did you ever sit down and plan a vacation for your family?

1131
01:28:43.448 --> 01:28:46.621
What we going to do this summer?

1132
01:28:46.621 --> 01:28:47.980
That's not life.

1133
01:28:47.980 --> 01:28:48.887
That's existing.

1134
01:28:48.887 --> 01:28:51.606
I guess it's good to exist.

1135
01:28:51.606 --> 01:28:56.138
You ain't got nothing else you can do I guess.

1136
01:28:56.138 --> 01:28:57.044
You exist.

1137
01:28:57.044 --> 01:29:02.483
Stayed there all my life never had a vacation with my family.

1138
01:29:02.483 --> 01:29:02.936
Never.

1139
01:29:02.936 --> 01:29:06.562
Say, "Well we going to go to . . ." Where?

1140
01:29:06.562 --> 01:29:09.281
Where do you go on vacation?

1141
01:29:09.281 --> 01:29:12.000
Never heard nothing like that.

1142
01:29:12.000 --> 01:29:15.333
<v ARSENEAULT>What would they do for fun?

1143
01:29:15.333 --> 01:29:15.889
<v SMITH>Huh?

1144
01:29:15.889 --> 01:29:19.222
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they do for fun?

1145
01:29:19.222 --> 01:29:23.111
<v SMITH>Give you a shovel and a hole.

1146
01:29:23.111 --> 01:29:27.000
Some other kind of work to do.

1147
01:29:27.000 --> 01:29:28.667
That's their fun.

1148
01:29:28.667 --> 01:29:29.778
Wasn't nothing.

1149
01:29:29.778 --> 01:29:33.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any musicians in the area?

1150
01:29:33.667 --> 01:29:34.222
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1151
01:29:34.222 --> 01:29:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Musicians in the area?

1152
01:29:37.000 --> 01:29:38.656
<v SMITH>You live here?

1153
01:29:38.656 --> 01:29:40.865
My son’s a musician.

1154
01:29:40.865 --> 01:29:48.042
That's the reason I ask you did you live . . . No, I don't believe.

1155
01:29:48.042 --> 01:29:52.458
They had some guys what they called musicians.

1156
01:29:52.458 --> 01:30:02.396
I don't know nobody here that . . . My type of musicians know [cutting?] a type of album or anything.

1157
01:30:02.396 --> 01:30:08.469
There always sitting, got somebody come in and play for them.

1158
01:30:08.469 --> 01:30:11.229
Entertain them in that situation.

1159
01:30:11.229 --> 01:30:16.750
Not just to say some guy . . . Now they got [Edward?].

1160
01:30:16.750 --> 01:30:22.823
One fellow here I know [Burdon?] I think his name was.

1161
01:30:22.823 --> 01:30:27.240
He was . . . But he didn't stay here long.

1162
01:30:27.240 --> 01:30:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did he play?

1163
01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:32.529
<v SMITH>He played pretty well anything.

1164
01:30:32.529 --> 01:30:43.153
Played an organ most of the time because . . . His daddy called himself a musician sitting there on a box playing something.

1165
01:30:43.153 --> 01:30:44.165
Picking guitar.

1166
01:30:44.165 --> 01:30:48.718
He never . . . Nothing ever came out of it financially.

1167
01:30:48.718 --> 01:30:54.788
No kind of fame or nothing like that came out of it.

1168
01:30:54.788 --> 01:30:55.800
That kind.

1169
01:30:55.800 --> 01:30:59.847
Actually a known musician that could bring some . . .

1170
01:30:59.847 --> 01:31:03.388
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there any zydeco music in Mossville?

1171
01:31:03.388 --> 01:31:03.894
<v SMITH>Yes.

1172
01:31:03.894 --> 01:31:10.471
Now I'm not up on . . . Yes they had . . . I'm not a zydeco person.

1173
01:31:10.471 --> 01:31:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>But they had it?

1174
01:31:13.000 --> 01:31:14.455
<v SMITH>Yes, they had it.

1175
01:31:14.455 --> 01:31:18.455
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you . . . Where could someone go to listen to it?

1176
01:31:18.455 --> 01:31:20.273
<v SMITH>Right here on Prater Road.

1177
01:31:20.273 --> 01:31:24.273
Right down the street there about a quarter of a mile.

1178
01:31:24.273 --> 01:31:27.182
Valery Montgomery had a club there and he . . .

1179
01:31:27.182 --> 01:31:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

1180
01:31:29.000 --> 01:31:29.420
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1181
01:31:29.420 --> 01:31:31.099
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the club?

1182
01:31:31.099 --> 01:31:33.198
Did it have a name?

1183
01:31:33.198 --> 01:31:37.397
<v SMITH>I don't . . . I never did know the name of it.

1184
01:31:37.397 --> 01:31:41.176
It had them zydecos on Saturday nights right there.

1185
01:31:41.176 --> 01:31:48.313
I wasn't living here I'd just come here and I been there because it carried a crowd.

1186
01:31:48.313 --> 01:31:51.252
I wanted to see what they're doing.

1187
01:31:51.252 --> 01:31:53.351
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it like there?

1188
01:31:53.351 --> 01:32:00.069
<v SMITH>Nothing but a . . . Just a bunch of . . . Too many people on the floor at one time.

1189
01:32:00.069 --> 01:32:00.908
<v ARSENEAULT>Doing what?

1190
01:32:00.908 --> 01:32:03.847
<v SMITH>Doing what they called zydeco I guess.

1191
01:32:03.847 --> 01:32:05.107
I never did.

1192
01:32:05.107 --> 01:32:06.366
I'm not zydeco.

1193
01:32:06.366 --> 01:32:08.466
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, it was a dance?

1194
01:32:08.466 --> 01:32:10.145
<v SMITH>Yes, that's a dance.

1195
01:32:10.145 --> 01:32:17.282
When you hear somebody down here talk about going to the zydeco . . . They played the longest music.

1196
01:32:17.282 --> 01:32:20.641
They played one piece of music thirty minutes.

1197
01:32:20.641 --> 01:32:24.000
Man, I get tired on that floor.

1198
01:32:24.000 --> 01:32:26.462
<v ARSENEAULT>How big was the place?

1199
01:32:26.462 --> 01:32:29.415
Like what did it look like?

1200
01:32:29.415 --> 01:32:32.862
<v SMITH>Oh it'd hold a couple hundred people.

1201
01:32:32.862 --> 01:32:33.846
Pretty big.

1202
01:32:33.846 --> 01:32:39.754
That many people on the floor at one time quite a square.

1203
01:32:39.754 --> 01:32:43.200
Takes up quite a bit of room.

1204
01:32:43.200 --> 01:32:45.169
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a bar?

1205
01:32:45.169 --> 01:32:47.631
<v SMITH>Yes, there was a bar.

1206
01:32:47.631 --> 01:32:51.569
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things could you get there?

1207
01:32:51.569 --> 01:32:52.062
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1208
01:32:52.062 --> 01:32:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of drinks could you get?

1209
01:32:56.000 --> 01:32:59.210
<v SMITH>It was just whatever you drink.

1210
01:32:59.210 --> 01:33:05.631
It wasn't a professional . . . a place where a professional bartender would work.

1211
01:33:05.631 --> 01:33:11.516
You want Jack Daniel's [whiskey] and coke let me have it.

1212
01:33:11.516 --> 01:33:15.796
That’s basically all I can say about it.

1213
01:33:15.796 --> 01:33:19.541
Didn't have that type of fancy name.

1214
01:33:19.541 --> 01:33:22.217
In fact, nowhere in here.

1215
01:33:22.217 --> 01:33:24.357
That's what you did.

1216
01:33:24.357 --> 01:33:40.943
You went to a bar here and you wanted a bottle or liquor you wanted to drink whatever you wanted to drink they give it to you a bottle and glass.

1217
01:33:40.943 --> 01:33:46.293
Some ice, coke, whatever you want to drink it with.

1218
01:33:46.293 --> 01:33:51.108
Nothing professional like what I think you're talking about.

1219
01:33:51.108 --> 01:33:54.854
Go to a bar and get a . . .

1220
01:33:54.854 --> 01:33:56.459
<v ARSENEAULT>I’m just curious.

1221
01:33:56.459 --> 01:33:56.994
<v SMITH>Yes.

1222
01:33:56.994 --> 01:33:59.669
<v ARSENEAULT>So like beer, hard liquor?

1223
01:33:59.669 --> 01:34:00.739
<v SMITH>Oh yes.

1224
01:34:00.739 --> 01:34:01.274
Beer.

1225
01:34:01.274 --> 01:34:03.949
Yes, beer and hard liquor.

1226
01:34:03.949 --> 01:34:10.369
That was every Friday night and every Saturday night on the town.

1227
01:34:10.369 --> 01:34:16.790
Then ready to go back to work as far as I know.

1228
01:34:16.790 --> 01:34:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you know Valery Montgomery?

1229
01:34:20.000 --> 01:34:21.935
<v SMITH>Yes, I knew him.

1230
01:34:21.935 --> 01:34:23.870
<v ARSENEAULT>What was he like?

1231
01:34:23.870 --> 01:34:25.804
<v SMITH>I mean at first.

1232
01:34:25.804 --> 01:34:37.897
Well he called himself [coughs] a businessman and I guess for where he was in this community and people he serving I guess he was.

1233
01:34:37.897 --> 01:34:41.766
In a black community that type of businessman.

1234
01:34:41.766 --> 01:34:45.636
He was always getting up something going on.

1235
01:34:45.636 --> 01:34:51.924
He wanted to be a politician and maybe he was a Mossville politician.

1236
01:34:51.924 --> 01:34:53.375
I don't know.

1237
01:34:53.375 --> 01:34:58.212
He always wanting to gather with something that's politically motivated.

1238
01:34:58.212 --> 01:35:03.049
And I don't think the man could read and write.

1239
01:35:03.049 --> 01:35:05.467
I'm just . . . I'm not sure.

1240
01:35:05.467 --> 01:35:06.918
I don't know.

1241
01:35:06.918 --> 01:35:10.304
I been told but I don't know.

1242
01:35:10.304 --> 01:35:15.625
I just knew him as the individual who stayed in Mossville.

1243
01:35:15.625 --> 01:35:23.848
I'd come from Houston here down here sometimes they'd have something going on over at his club.

1244
01:35:23.848 --> 01:35:33.038
I'd go in there looking for somebody I knew or seeing what they was doing or what have you.

1245
01:35:33.038 --> 01:35:38.359
Other than that I don't know a whole lot about it.

1246
01:35:38.359 --> 01:35:42.712
I don't even know where Valery Montgomery come from.

1247
01:35:42.712 --> 01:35:46.582
He migrated in here from somewhere down east.

1248
01:35:46.582 --> 01:35:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about his brother?

1249
01:35:49.000 --> 01:35:49.595
<v SMITH>Wes?

1250
01:35:49.595 --> 01:35:52.568
Called himself a businessman too.

1251
01:35:52.568 --> 01:35:56.135
I don't know what or what.

1252
01:35:56.135 --> 01:35:58.514
Don't know for what.

1253
01:35:58.514 --> 01:36:02.081
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he have a club too?

1254
01:36:02.081 --> 01:36:02.676
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1255
01:36:02.676 --> 01:36:06.243
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Wes have a club too?

1256
01:36:06.243 --> 01:36:07.432
<v SMITH>A what?

1257
01:36:07.432 --> 01:36:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>A club or a bar?

1258
01:36:11.000 --> 01:36:13.348
<v SMITH>Oh I don't know.

1259
01:36:13.348 --> 01:36:16.283
Not that I know of.

1260
01:36:16.283 --> 01:36:19.217
Not that I know of.

1261
01:36:19.217 --> 01:36:21.565
Now he may have.

1262
01:36:21.565 --> 01:36:24.500
That don't mean he didn't.

1263
01:36:24.500 --> 01:36:32.717
A lot of these clubs I come down here I didn't go to them.

1264
01:36:32.717 --> 01:36:39.761
Depends on how rough they look or who's doing what in there.

1265
01:36:39.761 --> 01:36:46.804
I didn't . . . I don't remember anybody telling me he had a club.

1266
01:36:46.804 --> 01:36:51.500
I don't want to say he didn't because . . .

1267
01:36:51.500 --> 01:36:53.848
<v ARSENEAULT>I was just curious.

1268
01:36:53.848 --> 01:36:59.717
I didn't know what kind of business he did or . . .

1269
01:36:59.717 --> 01:37:03.239
<v SMITH>Yes, he was always doing something.

1270
01:37:03.239 --> 01:37:12.630
I say at the end of where he's out there now he begin to do construction.

1271
01:37:12.630 --> 01:37:17.913
What he doing before then I really don't know.

1272
01:37:17.913 --> 01:37:21.435
It couldn't have been too much.

1273
01:37:21.435 --> 01:37:23.783
They all died broke.

1274
01:37:23.783 --> 01:37:25.543
Business is that.

1275
01:37:25.543 --> 01:37:27.304
Got no money.

1276
01:37:27.304 --> 01:37:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Anything else you remember about the club?

1277
01:37:32.000 --> 01:37:41.839
<v SMITH>No. I know a couple of times I passed a crowd, so he must have been doing something right.

1278
01:37:41.839 --> 01:37:44.946
Somebody liked it in the crowd.

1279
01:37:44.946 --> 01:37:48.571
<v ARSENEAULT>You remember any musicians who played there?

1280
01:37:48.571 --> 01:37:49.089
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1281
01:37:49.089 --> 01:37:51.679
<v ARSENEAULT>Any musicians who played there?

1282
01:37:51.679 --> 01:37:55.304
<v SMITH>No, I didn't know any of them.

1283
01:37:55.304 --> 01:38:01.000
It's . . . They had them musicians play that zydeco stuff there.

1284
01:38:01.000 --> 01:38:02.657
<v ARSENEAULT>But nobody in particular?

1285
01:38:02.657 --> 01:38:04.729
<v SMITH>No, I didn't know them.

1286
01:38:04.729 --> 01:38:10.943
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we're almost at two hours so I'm going to ask you one more question.

1287
01:38:10.943 --> 01:38:11.357
<v SMITH>Okay.

1288
01:38:11.357 --> 01:38:21.300
<v ARSENEAULT>And then we can wrap up and you can . . . We'll schedule a follow up with you because there's a lot of questions on here.

1289
01:38:21.300 --> 01:38:23.371
Something about a post office.

1290
01:38:23.371 --> 01:38:26.686
Was there ever a post office in Mossville?

1291
01:38:26.686 --> 01:38:28.343
That's not the question.

1292
01:38:28.343 --> 01:38:30.000
I'm just curious.

1293
01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:31.354
<v SMITH>Not in my time.

1294
01:38:31.354 --> 01:38:32.708
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever hear . . .

1295
01:38:32.708 --> 01:38:33.723
<v SMITH>But the . . . Pardon?

1296
01:38:33.723 --> 01:38:35.077
<v ARSENEAULT>You heard about one?

1297
01:38:35.077 --> 01:38:35.415
<v SMITH>Yes.

1298
01:38:35.415 --> 01:38:39.138
Not in my time, but there was a post office there.

1299
01:38:39.138 --> 01:38:41.169
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know where it was?

1300
01:38:41.169 --> 01:38:43.877
<v SMITH>It was over here by this railroad track.

1301
01:38:43.877 --> 01:38:47.600
On the west side of the railroad tracks so I'm told.

1302
01:38:47.600 --> 01:38:49.631
James Moss was the post master.

1303
01:38:49.631 --> 01:38:50.985
We call him Jim.

1304
01:38:50.985 --> 01:38:52.000
Jim Moss.

1305
01:38:52.000 --> 01:38:53.091
<v ARSENEAULT>You knew him?

1306
01:38:53.091 --> 01:38:54.909
<v SMITH>Oh yes, I knew him.

1307
01:38:54.909 --> 01:38:57.818
I don't know if he knows me though.

1308
01:38:57.818 --> 01:38:59.636
But yes, I knew him.

1309
01:38:59.636 --> 01:39:00.364
James Moss.

1310
01:39:00.364 --> 01:39:01.818
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he live?

1311
01:39:01.818 --> 01:39:02.182
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1312
01:39:02.182 --> 01:39:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he live?

1313
01:39:04.000 --> 01:39:11.862
<v SMITH>He lived back up here on the other side of Prater Road.

1314
01:39:11.862 --> 01:39:13.172
There's a

1315
01:39:13.172 --> 01:39:20.379
<c.p>Baptist church up there on the right side of the road.

1316
01:39:20.379 --> 01:39:26.931
He lived on the south side of the road there.

1317
01:39:26.931 --> 01:39:41.345
All that property around there now that you go from the gully near the south side of the road, that was his.

1318
01:39:41.345 --> 01:39:42.655
Jim Moss.

1319
01:39:42.655 --> 01:39:47.241
He had a son named Pete Moss.

1320
01:39:47.241 --> 01:39:49.862
He was just there.

1321
01:39:49.862 --> 01:40:11.483
He wasn't worth nothing. [laughs] What I . . . What my mother told me about this post office was that when he died nobody come to take over the business so to speak . . . run it.

1322
01:40:11.483 --> 01:40:17.379
I don’t know the details in the mail distribution.

1323
01:40:17.379 --> 01:40:20.000
It stayed there.

1324
01:40:20.000 --> 01:40:21.756
Mossville had a potential.

1325
01:40:21.756 --> 01:40:27.025
Like I said, so many people running the business with no education.

1326
01:40:27.025 --> 01:40:32.734
I have . . . I look at that . . . I frown on that quite a bit.

1327
01:40:32.734 --> 01:40:37.125
You running a business . . . You need to tell me something.

1328
01:40:37.125 --> 01:40:38.003
You educated?

1329
01:40:38.003 --> 01:40:42.833
You need to be able to help me with something here.

1330
01:40:42.833 --> 01:40:49.419
You got a bunch of uneducated people running something, that's a dangerous thing to me.

1331
01:40:49.419 --> 01:40:52.054
I don't know about other people.

1332
01:40:52.054 --> 01:41:00.397
You won't find me attending nothing like that too often because it's nothing good can come out of it.

1333
01:41:00.397 --> 01:41:04.348
Nobody knows who you are or what you're about.

1334
01:41:04.348 --> 01:41:05.666
That's not good.

1335
01:41:05.666 --> 01:41:12.252
I was going to tell you about what my mother told me about the potential.

1336
01:41:12.252 --> 01:41:14.887
Mossville should have a police department.

1337
01:41:14.887 --> 01:41:17.521
Just like the city of Westlake.

1338
01:41:17.521 --> 01:41:20.595
They should be run by people here.

1339
01:41:20.595 --> 01:41:21.473
What happened?

1340
01:41:21.473 --> 01:41:26.742
Everybody wants to be the chief nobody want to be the Indian.

1341
01:41:26.742 --> 01:41:28.059
That's bottom line.

1342
01:41:28.059 --> 01:41:28.938
Bottom line.

1343
01:41:28.938 --> 01:41:35.963
Don't care what these people in Mossville start out and it might be a good thing.

1344
01:41:35.963 --> 01:41:38.159
And this, I'm watching this.

1345
01:41:38.159 --> 01:41:45.184
And this . . . If the head honcho can't have his ways you . . . Rigmaiden, Williams, whatever he is.

1346
01:41:45.184 --> 01:41:49.575
It's not going to come to nothing good in Mossville.

1347
01:41:49.575 --> 01:41:54.844
That might not be what you're looking for, but that's the truth.

1348
01:41:54.844 --> 01:41:57.918
Not going to come to nothing good.

1349
01:41:57.918 --> 01:42:03.187
Sooner or later you'll see it just fizzle out to just nothing.

1350
01:42:03.187 --> 01:42:05.822
You'll wonder where that fellow went.

1351
01:42:05.822 --> 01:42:09.773
"Oh he went on up there with somebody else.

1352
01:42:09.773 --> 01:42:11.530
Done some other business."

1353
01:42:11.530 --> 01:42:18.116
No. You have to have a leader in business to accomplish anything that's worth anything.

1354
01:42:18.116 --> 01:42:19.873
But if I don't

1355
01:42:19.873 --> 01:42:26.459
<c.p>want to follow you and you don't know what you’re . . . "Which way to Lake Charles?"

1356
01:42:26.459 --> 01:42:27.776
"Oh this way."

1357
01:42:27.776 --> 01:42:33.924
I ain't follow you, and you don't know how to get to Lake Charles.

1358
01:42:33.924 --> 01:42:35.680
You in bad shape.

1359
01:42:35.680 --> 01:42:37.875
That's the way Mossville is.

1360
01:42:37.875 --> 01:42:47.096
And when one finds one that's intelligent enough to get some help to work on the situation they pull him down.

1361
01:42:47.096 --> 01:42:52.365
They don't want that because they want nothing but what they got.

1362
01:42:52.365 --> 01:42:52.805
Nothing.

1363
01:42:52.805 --> 01:42:55.000
That's what they want.

1364
01:42:55.000 --> 01:42:59.740
I bet you half of Mossville done worked as a public worker here.

1365
01:42:59.740 --> 01:43:00.833
I just looked.

1366
01:43:00.833 --> 01:43:07.396
I passed look and I stopped sat on the tree there and look at them and see who.

1367
01:43:07.396 --> 01:43:08.854
Where'd he come from?

1368
01:43:08.854 --> 01:43:10.677
Oh he working for parish.

1369
01:43:10.677 --> 01:43:12.865
Well how'd he get that job?

1370
01:43:12.865 --> 01:43:16.510
Fellow I've been here for I don't know how long.

1371
01:43:16.510 --> 01:43:18.333
I don't approve of that.

1372
01:43:18.333 --> 01:43:20.156
I don't hold with that.

1373
01:43:20.156 --> 01:43:21.615
I don't do it.

1374
01:43:21.615 --> 01:43:27.448
You need to come together and elect who you want to do things on their qualifications.

1375
01:43:27.448 --> 01:43:33.646
If you don’t, you’re going to get messed up, especially if you got money involved in it.

1376
01:43:33.646 --> 01:43:39.115
You going nowhere because as soon as those few dollars play out you got nothing.

1377
01:43:39.115 --> 01:43:40.573
Where this fellow going?

1378
01:43:40.573 --> 01:43:43.490
Soon as your money is gone he's gone.

1379
01:43:43.490 --> 01:43:45.312
Can't do nothing for you.

1380
01:43:45.312 --> 01:43:47.500
You can't do nothing for him.

1381
01:43:47.500 --> 01:43:49.323
Can't do nothing for him.

1382
01:43:49.323 --> 01:43:53.698
You need . . . Most of them looking for help they need help themselves.

1383
01:43:53.698 --> 01:43:56.979
They need to be educated to what you're doing.

1384
01:43:56.979 --> 01:44:03.906
Sit down and come up with something that you never sat down and studied to see how it goes.

1385
01:44:03.906 --> 01:44:06.094
What’s ifs and ands in it?.

1386
01:44:06.094 --> 01:44:19.219
Ain't going to get nowhere there because you going to come to a hard place in the road where you make a decision that's going to cost you everything you doing if you make that decision.

1387
01:44:19.219 --> 01:44:20.312
Just don't work.

1388
01:44:20.312 --> 01:44:21.771
So I don't know.

1389
01:44:21.771 --> 01:44:24.323
I was talking about Jim Moss here.

1390
01:44:24.323 --> 01:44:27.240
Talking about Mossville here for what they had.

1391
01:44:27.240 --> 01:44:34.167
Mossville could have been something, but if it ain't who they want in Mossville it's not going to work.

1392
01:44:34.167 --> 01:44:35.990
Do everything you can but . . .

1393
01:44:35.990 --> 01:44:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did your mom tell you about the post office?

1394
01:44:40.000 --> 01:44:43.386
<v SMITH>Oh, she said it just went down.

1395
01:44:43.386 --> 01:44:47.739
Just did nothing about it after James Moss died.

1396
01:44:47.739 --> 01:44:50.641
They didn't have another postmaster general.

1397
01:44:50.641 --> 01:45:04.667
And my conclusion is what she was saying nobody have sense enough to come down here and run the post office and distribute the mail and pick it up.

1398
01:45:04.667 --> 01:45:09.987
I don't believe you need to be educated to do that.

1399
01:45:09.987 --> 01:45:20.627
That's rules and things you need to know according to the postal rules, but I believe anybody can dispatch a mail truck.

1400
01:45:20.627 --> 01:45:23.529
It's so easy because it's Mossville.

1401
01:45:23.529 --> 01:45:26.431
Because we know everybody in Mossville.

1402
01:45:26.431 --> 01:45:30.301
Ain't even got to study who lives where.

1403
01:45:30.301 --> 01:45:31.752
"Who this here?

1404
01:45:31.752 --> 01:45:33.203
What's his address?"

1405
01:45:33.203 --> 01:45:35.621
You know everybody in Mossville.

1406
01:45:35.621 --> 01:45:40.941
Should be just that easy, but that's not what it is.

1407
01:45:40.941 --> 01:45:44.327
They all like a bunch of crabs.

1408
01:45:44.327 --> 01:45:46.745
They all against one another.

1409
01:45:46.745 --> 01:45:59.804
When it comes to something ninety-five percent of the time Mossville's too late because they didn't get the information because they didn't tell but ten of them.

1410
01:45:59.804 --> 01:46:01.739
Yeah that's the clique.

1411
01:46:01.739 --> 01:46:06.092
You ain't in it you gets none of it.

1412
01:46:06.092 --> 01:46:09.477
That's really been my experience with Mossville.

1413
01:46:09.477 --> 01:46:10.928
You said what?

1414
01:46:10.928 --> 01:46:12.379
I'm not Mossville.

1415
01:46:12.379 --> 01:46:14.314
Never have never will.

1416
01:46:14.314 --> 01:46:21.085
I'll be a citizen of the city, but I won't be a Mossville person.

1417
01:46:21.085 --> 01:46:26.889
I've never been into nothing that they put on that's worth anything.

1418
01:46:26.889 --> 01:46:35.595
Most of the time what they trying to put over you the last one going to get it.

1419
01:46:35.595 --> 01:46:45.752
You don't know how to study it or what it is and anything like that because that clique or that group.

1420
01:46:45.752 --> 01:46:55.425
Don't put it out there where you can study it or you can look at it and give your opinion.

1421
01:46:55.425 --> 01:47:01.229
My opinion don't mean nothing then what am I in here for?

1422
01:47:01.229 --> 01:47:06.065
Mossville has been this way for eighty-one years to me.

1423
01:47:06.065 --> 01:47:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean cliquish?

1424
01:47:08.000 --> 01:47:08.515
<v SMITH>Yes.

1425
01:47:08.515 --> 01:47:12.636
I don't believe it'll ever be anything different.

1426
01:47:12.636 --> 01:47:19.333
That's the wrong attitude, but I got a reason for feeling like that.

1427
01:47:19.333 --> 01:47:23.970
I think everything going to do better sooner or

1428
01:47:23.970 --> 01:47:24.485
<c.p>later.

1429
01:47:24.485 --> 01:47:26.545
Most of the time.

1430
01:47:26.545 --> 01:47:29.121
They sit in the situation.

1431
01:47:29.121 --> 01:47:33.758
They're satisfied with this bunch and who they are.

1432
01:47:33.758 --> 01:47:35.303
That's good enough.

1433
01:47:35.303 --> 01:47:38.394
Kind of like a little club.

1434
01:47:38.394 --> 01:47:42.000
You don't ever take any new members,

1435
01:47:42.000 --> 01:47:52.818
you going to wind up with a bunch of people that can't help you out that don't know what's going on.

1436
01:47:52.818 --> 01:48:00.545
Got to have new blood, new life to a situation to get something of it.

1437
01:48:00.545 --> 01:48:02.606
Mossville ain't really nothing.

1438
01:48:02.606 --> 01:48:05.697
Mossville ain't really into extend themselves.

1439
01:48:05.697 --> 01:48:06.727
They're full.

1440
01:48:06.727 --> 01:48:08.273
I don't know.

1441
01:48:08.273 --> 01:48:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I was going to ask you if you had any fond memories of Mossville.

1442
01:48:16.000 --> 01:48:17.030
<v SMITH>Any what?

1443
01:48:17.030 --> 01:48:18.060
<v ARSENEAULT>Fond memories.

1444
01:48:18.060 --> 01:48:27.328
<v SMITH>Not really, because eighteen and on, that extent, I've . . . My life I was gone from here and up.

1445
01:48:27.328 --> 01:48:38.657
I know better memories of some things that I could ponder my mind on than think of what Mossville would have had,

1446
01:48:38.657 --> 01:48:48.440
had I stayed here I don't . . . It's strange that they would ask me to come up here and talk.

1447
01:48:48.440 --> 01:48:50.500
I talk to [T.

1448
01:48:50.500 --> 01:49:03.888
Porter?] and that's the reason I'm here because I told him I don't know what you want but I told him whenever he talked to me,

1449
01:49:03.888 --> 01:49:08.007
"I'm going to answer what they ask me."

1450
01:49:08.007 --> 01:49:11.612
<v ARSENEAULT>We just want to get the full . . .

1451
01:49:11.612 --> 01:49:21.396
<v SMITH>I said, I'm going to build something that ain't there and paint Mossville up to be what it ain't.

1452
01:49:21.396 --> 01:49:25.000
I ain't going to do that.

1453
01:49:25.000 --> 01:49:27.817
<v ARSENEAULT>We want to get everybody's story.

1454
01:49:27.817 --> 01:49:28.287
<v SMITH>Yes.

1455
01:49:28.287 --> 01:49:31.104
I ain't going to do that.

1456
01:49:31.104 --> 01:49:33.922
It's either right or it's wrong.

1457
01:49:33.922 --> 01:49:34.391
Wrong.

1458
01:49:34.391 --> 01:49:40.026
These children see you coming what you think they going to do?

1459
01:49:40.026 --> 01:49:41.435
The same thing.

1460
01:49:41.435 --> 01:49:44.722
"Well this is what coach so-and-so did.

1461
01:49:44.722 --> 01:49:48.009
This is what . . ." Nothing out of that.

1462
01:49:48.009 --> 01:49:50.357
Not going to do that.

1463
01:49:50.357 --> 01:49:53.174
I wouldn't do it to Mossville.

1464
01:49:53.174 --> 01:49:58.809
It may never be nothing, but it won't be because of me.

1465
01:49:58.809 --> 01:50:00.217
You from here?

1466
01:50:00.217 --> 01:50:00.687
Where?

1467
01:50:00.687 --> 01:50:03.504
<v ARSENEAULT>No, I'm from Baton Rouge [Louisiana].

1468
01:50:03.504 --> 01:50:06.791
<v SMITH>You ought to hear my son play.

1469
01:50:06.791 --> 01:50:07.730
<v ARSENEAULT>He's good?

1470
01:50:07.730 --> 01:50:08.670
<v SMITH>He's good.

1471
01:50:08.670 --> 01:50:10.548
<v ARSENEAULT>When did he learn?

1472
01:50:10.548 --> 01:50:11.017
<v SMITH>Pardon?

1473
01:50:11.017 --> 01:50:12.896
<v ARSENEAULT>When did he learn?

1474
01:50:12.896 --> 01:50:15.243
Or how did he learn?

1475
01:50:15.243 --> 01:50:19.000
<v SMITH>Oh he went to McNeese [State University].

1476
01:50:19.000 --> 01:50:19.982
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

1477
01:50:19.982 --> 01:50:21.945
What does he play?

1478
01:50:21.945 --> 01:50:22.927
<v SMITH>Plays saxophone.

1479
01:50:22.927 --> 01:50:23.418
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1480
01:50:23.418 --> 01:50:23.909
Saxophone.

1481
01:50:23.909 --> 01:50:25.382
What's his name?

1482
01:50:25.382 --> 01:50:27.345
<v SMITH>Mickey, just like mine.

1483
01:50:27.345 --> 01:50:28.327
Same thing.

1484
01:50:28.327 --> 01:50:32.255
<v ARSENEAULT>He's your . . . How many children do you have?

1485
01:50:32.255 --> 01:50:32.745
<v SMITH>Two.

1486
01:50:32.745 --> 01:50:33.236
<v ARSENEAULT>Two.

1487
01:50:33.236 --> 01:50:36.182
<v SMITH>She's waiting for you out there.

1488
01:50:36.182 --> 01:50:36.673
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1489
01:50:36.673 --> 01:50:40.109
Well thank you for your time today.

1490
01:50:40.109 --> 01:50:40.600
<v SMITH>Alright.

1491
01:50:40.600 --> 01:50:45.018
<v ARSENEAULT>Would you be interested in doing a follow up?

1492
01:50:45.018 --> 01:50:46.000
<v SMITH>Yes.

1493
01:50:46.000 --> 01:50:50.475
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, because I have a couple more questions we didn't really get to.

1494
01:50:50.475 --> 01:50:51.164
<v SMITH>I would.

1495
01:50:51.164 --> 01:50:54.607
Somebody said I could get it if I wanted to.

1496
01:50:54.607 --> 01:50:54.951
After . . .

1497
01:50:54.951 --> 01:50:55.639
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh my . . .

1498
01:50:55.639 --> 01:50:55.984
<v SMITH>Yes.

1499
01:50:55.984 --> 01:50:57.016
<v ARSENEAULT>Our contact info?

1500
01:50:57.016 --> 01:51:00.803
Yes, I'll give you my card and you can contact us.

1501
01:51:00.803 --> 01:51:01.148
<v SMITH>Okay.

1502
01:51:01.148 --> 01:51:04.590
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you so much and we'll be in touch.

1503
01:51:04.590 --> 01:51:04.934
<v SMITH>Alright.

1504
01:51:04.934 --> 01:51:05.967
What's your name?

1505
01:51:05.967 --> 01:51:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm Chelsea.

1506
01:51:07.000 --> 01:51:07.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4534.

1507
01:51:07.000 --> 01:51:07.200
End Session I.]
